Erik Corry <ec@mailhost.sign-tronic.dk> writes:

> > Because it is a relatively simple design, and they a very good fab process
> > (I checked with a former DEC employee on this, and he says I'm correct)
> > they can get high clock rates.  The trade off with work per clock cycle
> 
> They will still have a very good fab process in the future.
> Intel and Samsung. It's as good as it gets.

This is very true, and too often the anti-RISC, or more importantly,
the anti-non-Intel types don't get it.  The design costs of the Alpha
(or just about any cpu chip) were much much less than that of the
Pentium family.  The Pentium is fast because of boggling amounts of
resources put into its design.

Now imagine an Alpha designed with the same budget.  Sounds yummy.

An important win of RISC these days is that the costs of designing
CISC is too high.  The market for any cpu chip that isn't coming from
Intel is very small, no matter how superior the chip.  Thus a cheaper
design cycle is critical.

