Hi,

Our group is considering buying some DEC XL300 Alpha pc/workstations.
These machines are very cheap (~$800) and have good fp benchmarks, but 
they come with only 32MB of ram out of the box. The spec sheets say that
the memory used is "industry standard SIMMs", but when we have inquired
as to memory prices from various places, we get prices of ~$700 for 
128MB (4 by 32 MB), which is what we would like to add to each machine.
This nearly doubles the price of the machine, and seems very expensive
for memory. When I asked one of the vendors why it was so expensive, I
was told that in this case the industry standard was tier one memory,
which is more expensive and is required by DEC.

So, the questions are - what is the difference between tier one memory
and the generic simms I see advertised for dirt cheap and are the tier
one chips really necessary? Has anyone tried putting generic 72 pin 
simms in one of these machines?

Please cc responses to:

warren@ricci.phys.uwm.edu.

Thanks,
Warren
