In article <351d387f.0@blushng.jps.net>, "Gary Hedberg" <gary@hedberg.org> writes:
:I'm in the process of buying an older DEC workstation.  The guy says it's a
:Multi 166, but in checking on DEC's web page there is no mention of any
:"Multia"  Does anyone know if this is the same as an AS 200/166?

  The Multia series was an early "Network PC", and was a way for sites
  to use and manage large numbers of networked configurations of Windows
  NT systems -- the Multia fit between the contemporary views of a
  network-based workstation, and the views of a PC with a network card.
  Particular emphasis was placed on the (remote) management of Multia.

  The Multia is not an AlphaStation 200 4/166 -- despite using the same
  basic Alpha microprocessor running at the same basic MHz rating.

  Multia was supported for Windows NT only.  OpenVMS is not supported.
  (This for administrative decisions, and not for technical reasons).
  If memory serves, one can get DIGITAL UNIX and Linux to bootstrap
  and run on the Multia.

