Tim. Bluck. <tb@oes-ee.demon.co.uk> writes:
>         I have recently  aquired a Vax 11/750.   After initial checks
>         I powered the system up,   I found that the   "reg-fail"  LED
>         on the power controller was lit,   I traced this over  to the
>         2.5V power sypply assembly   where I found that the   +/-5V &
>         +12V rails are not working. Has anyone had this fault before?
>         The power supply doesn't look to complicated.   But as I only 
>         have a block diagram, and not a full circuit for it I thought
>         I'd ask first..

  The printset should still be available from DEC, or someone on the net
might give you one. I'd suggest trying to scrounge another supply, though.
You should be able to get one quite cheaply (at least here in the US) if
you don't appear too eager (the price for obsolete stuff varies greatly
based on the perceived level of buyer urgency).

>         I'll  also be needing a boot tape for this system  as it only
>         has it's internal tape drive and no obvious way of connecting
>         to An  external drive of any kind.

  That tape holds a trivial amount of storage (256K maybe - I forget). DEC
used to update the 750 microcode by shipping new ROMs. Eventually they gave
up (there are a *huge* number of microcode revs on the 750) and loaded the
microcode patches from tape. Similarly, instead of shipping new boot ROMs
and dealing with the on-disk boot format that was peculiar to the 750, they
put VMB (the VMS bootstrap) on the tape.

  So it's quite possible that a tape is not needed at all (or is optional)
on your system. There's a microcode update utility that can be run from VMS
to update the microcode once booted - you only needed the tape if your ex-
isting microcode wasn't good enough to boot from a specific device (like
the CI cluster adapter).

  Again, those tapes are still available from DEC, or perhaps someone on the
net can give you one they're not using.

>	  Also the system only has
>         one serial port which I assume is for the console.  
>         Was this a standard configuration? One tape drive and One RS-
>         232 port would be fairly useless wouldn't it?

  Sounds like you have a processor only. There are a great number of add-on
devices available, but these were configured to match customer requirements.
Take a look on the left side of the card cage (where the smaller boards are)
and see if there are any device controllers there.

