It was a few years ago that I did this, but there are serial to parallel
convertion boxes around. These allow you to connet printers that only have
parallel ports.

As for changing font sizes, just look up the control codes in the manuals,
and put them into simple text files, place these text files into a text
library. When you create the terminal printer queues you specify the library
name, and reset forms...

It is then easy to have symbols which equate to things like:

wprint :== print/que=hpdesk500 /form=wide
landscape :== print/que=hpdesk500 /form=landscape

Where the /form=<names> are the names of the files placed into the text
library. I have used this setup with DestJet 500 and HP LaserJets in the
past. Although we had these printers hanging off of DECserver's so that
multiple machines could access them.

I'm sorry that I can't give you instructions on setting up printer queues on
serial lines, but it has been a long time since I did that, and I am nowhere
near a VMS system at this moment to look it up. I'm not certain but I think
there was an example command proceedure in sys$examples: to set up a printer
on a terminal port.


