>Alpha, like most new RISC machines, is a load/store architecture.  Only a
few
>load/store instructions are affected.  It's a fairly simple matter to
implement
>this in some parallel data path with a set of gates, one of which selects a
>"stright through" wiring on the parallel path, and one of which selects a
>"rearrange" wiring on the parallel path.  The exact details of how Alpha,
MIPS,
>PowerPC, or HP-PARC 7100 bi-endian chips select little-endian or big-endian
>mode are unknown to me, but simple to conceive.
>


I didn't see how this therad started, but it does not seem very relevent as
VMS, NT, DEC Unix, and I think Linux are all little endian on Alpha anyway.
(The same as VAX and Intel hardware.)

On the changing mode issue, the MIPS R3000 and later hardware can
potentially change the endian setting per process, however I'm not sure if
Alpha goes that far.

Laurence Blunt






