> > Except that the market for Alphas will fade away once IA-64 is here.

That's what the press says. That's what a lot of analysts and
consultants say. The ironic thing is that Intel has convinced Key
Influencers and damn near everyone else that IA64 is the answer to
everything. An architecture that has yet to exist in silicon has
won the hearts and minds of all and sundry.

OK, let's assume that everyone and his brother are building IA64-based
enterprise servers in 2002 or so. Assuming that it maintains and extends
its performance leadership, Alpha will remain very viable as a high-end
differentiator. Twice the speed of everyone else's solution will be a
compelling proof point.

The rest of the RISC market is toast. MIPs will succeed only in set-top
boxes and embedded apps (not a bad market). PA-RISC was taken out of
play by the IA64 deal (smart move, Andy... co-opt a competitor). You
don't hear much about PowerPC these days (more embedded apps, I
suspect). 88K is dead. Sun still has Great Expectations for SPARC, and
while a prudent individual does not bet against Scott McNealy, note that
Sun is porting Solaris to IA64.

Alpha's biggest problem is Digital's utterly squatulent marketing
thereof. It's a pity that the firm elected to produce a kick-butt
architecture and then bend over backwards to clad it in Stealth, no,
Romulan Cloaking Technology.

