Boy isn't that the truth!!  I remember speaking with Blaise Fanning, the
Chief Techologist at Deskstation, after they booted up their first 21164
.  (Deskstation, like Carrera, used Mips previously).

"This is the fasted thing that we have ever seen!  It blew us away!"
was  my voice mail from Blaise.

Alpha, is a great Microprocessor.  DEC, unfortunatly, had other problems
to deal with rather than promote Alpha.  I think what doomed DEC, was
it's dual architecture support (Intel and Alpha, in the systems group)
and not allowing Digital Semi to be completely seperate unit.  (I know,
I know, Digital Semi is a seperate business unit, but when it came to
Unix chip pricing versus NT chip pricing, this to me, showed that the
systems group had some "clout" with the Semi group marketing).  Plus
Palmer had to show a profit, or raise the stock price, whichever
happened first. 

Although we can second guess this to death, time will tell how Alpha
really does in the market place!  Heck, maybe Digital Semi should have
found some VC money.  If they were totally an independent unit, perhaps
we would have seen Alpha support at other large companies.  (I know this
might spark (sorry no pun intended at Sun!) some controversy).  I do
have the utmost respect for Digital Semi, it's technology and it's
people.  They are extremely gifted and dedicated individuals.

