In article <351460D6.C20A915@cerritos.edu>, lakha001@cerritos.edu writes:
>Can someone help me out with this. I am trying to write a simple C
>program on DEC/Alpha system. For some reason, it just won't compile. I
>am not sure what I am missing out. I looked at the HELP pages on the
>system and the CC run time routines do show popen as a command.
>
>$ CC/DECC/PREFIX_LIBRARY_ENTRIES=ALL_ENTRIE New.c
>
>        fi = popen("PMDF MAIL", "w");
>.............^
>%CC-I-IMPLICITFUNC, In this statement, the identifier "popen" is
>implicitly decl
>ared as a function.
>at line number 6 in file FOO_BAR:[MY.LAKHA001]NEW.C;2
>

You need a later version of VMS.  popen is new for VMS 7.0, and stdio.h knows
that.  If you're using a 6.x you may be able to use the "backport" kit which
gets you the new C library for older vesions than 7.0.  stdio.h may or may not
be updated by the backport kit, you'll have to check (only the copy in
sys$library:decc$rtldef.tlb is actually used).

