wilson@dbit.com (John Wilson) writes:

>PMI machines confuse things more because (AFAIK) they don't even try to
>access PMI memory unless the address is below the I/O page in their opinion.
>But presumably you could still stick in 4 KB of Q-bus memory in the first
>half of the I/O page, again assuming you could convince it to ignore BBS7
>and respond to I/O page addresses (it would be slower than the rest of
>memory though).

In reading the 11/83 manual, I have found paragraphs describing how
non-I/O page addresses are fixed below 17760000 and addresses above
this point are separated into 'internal' I/O page addresses,
'external' I/O page addresses and CPU register addresses.

External I/O page addresses are those supplied by other options.
Internal I/O page addresses are those supplied by the KDJ11-B, and
CPU registers are those in a specific range (which I can't quote
since I don't have the manual immediately available).

I tried using a KDJ11-B processor board and an MSV11-DD strapped to
respond to bank7 addresses.  What I found was that the memory (all
of it) showed up in a 'MAP', which is what I would expect since it
really is physical memory between 000000 and 177776.  But when I
booted RT-11 SB, it was only able to see up to 157776.  This is in
keeping with the forced 4kw I/O page...

