In <Eq6ytE.850@world.std.com> Megan wrote:
| Jerome Fine <jhfine@idirect.com> writes:
| 
| >I have an old DEC memory board M8044 (64 KBytes)
| >which allows for a wire wrap of an I/O page of 2 KWords.
| >I tried this on a friend's system using a quad 11/73 in a
| >BA23 box.  I ran RT11SJ and did a "SHOW  MEMORY"
| >which showed the top of memory to be 160000 rather
| >than 170000.  Just in case the monitor was unable to
| >use the memory between 160000 and 167776, I also
| >ran ODT an tried to access the addresses in the range
| >above 160000.  I was disappointed to find that software
| >ODT could not find any memory above 160000.
| 
| Having just checked this out recently for someone, I can
| tell you that the documentation specifically states that
| the I/O page is 8kb (4kw), no mention at all of being
| able to have it set 4kb (2kw).

Let's try to remember the hardware and software context in which
the 2kW I/O page first came about.  A simple LSI-11 (11/03) system
with 16-bit address space and no memory management.

In that situation, a 2kW increase in available memory, from 28kW
to 30kW, was really significant.  RT-11 could use it, we could run
bigger programs, etc.  The only down side was having to rewire
some peripheral controllers to have addresses above the 170000 boundary.
It just worked.

In later days, available memory space increased to a huge 248kB or 4MB
and a 4kB memory increment was a much smaller relative gain.

    carl

