In article <6f5v83$lne@post.gsfc.nasa.gov>, koehler@bessta.gsfc.nasa.aspm.gov (Bob Koehler) writes:
|> 
|> In article <3512C0E0.1763@NO-SPAM.sgi.net>, Adam Stouffer <tesla@NO-SPAM.sgi.net> writes:
|> >Can anyone tell me some info about a DECstation 3100? Can I use a normal
|> >PC monitor on it? What type of ram does it use? What software does it
|> >run? Thanks
|> >
|> 
|> A DECstation 3100 is a MIPS R3000 processor set up to run DEC's ULTRIX, a since
|> retired version of BSD UNIX (mostly 4.2 with some 4.3).  I used to have one.

Having written the CPU-ident code for two kernels, I can safely say
that the 16Mhz R2000a microprocessor used in the KN01 claim to have an
r3000 core.  Still has the R2000 pin interface and external cache
interface.  Real r3000s have sane writer buffers and less external
circuitry.

And no, you can't use a normal PC monitor on it. You need a multisync
monitor that can do sync-on-green.

It runs Ultrix, Mach, (if you can find it), unreleased OSF/1 versions,
and 4.4BSD derivatives.  If you want a non-Unix environment, forget it.

Many people are happy with NetBSD.  Others unaccountably prefer
OpenBSD.  A Linux port has been coming RSN since, oh, before I cobbled
together the first NetBSD/pmax bootblocks.  Either NetBSD or OpenBSD
runs the typical range of free-Unix software, which probably builds
better on a more modern OS than it does on Ultrix.

The RAM is a DEC-standard (read: nonstandard) 80-pin 2meg SIMMs.
You can install them in pairs up to 24 Mbytes.

These things are fun toys and a great way to learn about BSD Unix, and
a bare working system should go for about $50.  Compare to a 486 and
make your choice.



