In article <kqq6wCANt8E1Ewr$@csking.demon.co.uk>,
  Chris King <chris@csking.demon.co.uk> wrote:

> I've got a number of DEC PrintServer 17/600's dotted around a campus,
> currently operating over DECnet - I'd like to convert these to run
> over IP, and the manuals say that this should be possible.

By "operating," do you mean booting or printing?  PrintServer printers
can boot from OpenVMS systems via DECnet/MOP only.  If you want to boot
the printer via TCP/IP, you'll have to do it with Unix or Windows NT.

Once the printer is booted, you can print via both DECnet and TCP/IP if
you have given the printer both addresses.  The boot host does not have
to be running both protocols.

> However, another requirement is that the printers will have to sit in
> different subnets to the server (AlphaServer 2000 running OpenVMS7.1,
> DCPS 1.3, UCX 4.1 ECO 7 and Pathworks 5.0F) - is this actually
> possible? My main worry is that the printers won't initialise properly
> if moved to a routed subnet.

PrintServer printers boot over different subnets just fine, as long as
you are running PrintServer Software V5.1.

