In article <3515E215.222A@world.std.com>, Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>>You go first, I'll stay here with my VMS.  :-)
>
>
>Given my choice, so would I. OTOH, let's say you've got one box, and one
>box only due to floor space or budgetary limitations, whatever. Let's
>say that you have mission-critical apps that run on OVMS, but that you
>could save big bucks by running other apps in a less-costly environment
>such as WNT, or that you could run other apps that are not supported
>under OVMS. OS stability and resiliency issues aside, this makes a case
>for a mixed-OS environment.
> 

Hmm, I suspect that the financial argument here is suspect - the high end
machine we're talking about is going to cost a lot, so it would probably be 
cheaper to have a smaller one and a separate NT/Intel machine.  Still,
if the only additional costs are for:

 1. NT server license 
 2. NT specific disks

then, yes, adding NT this way would be cost effective.  If, on the other 
hand, there is some additional "galaxy OS slot license", then Digital
would probably set it at $10000, or something equally outrageous, and then
you'd be better off buying a separate NT machine.

Digital might actually have something here though, if the incremental
costs are reasonable.  For instance, a lot of nets need one NT machine
for WINS, domain controllers, and so forth, and little more.  Be nice to
just drop in one extra processor and fire up NT, without having to have a
separate box. Historically though, "reasonable cost" is not something I
expect Digital to deliver, and since you can set up a small NT machine to 
handle this task for under $2K, I would really be amazed if Digital could
beat that price point.

