In article <1998Mar21.074816.1@eisner>, kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) writes:

>I don't see how that follows at all.  If DEC Unix were big-endian, it
>could run on Alpha in the same box with Windows NT and VMS using the
>same techniques as in the demos.  Big-endian vs. Little-endian on
>Alpha is a matter of how you use the instruction set, all done in
>software (the compilers, to be specific).  

According to the architecture reference, one must choose big-endian or
little-endian from console mode before booting the OS on an Alpha.  You could
run both big-endian and little-endian OS on the same machine at different
times.  But that's a bit more than in "the compilers".  The compiler needs
to match the target mode, but you can't "hot-swap" to a different mode while
the CPU is running.  More importantly, you'ld have some difficulty coordinating
access to the I/O subsystem and such between two CPU's running different OS in
different modes.  Not that it couldn't be done.

