Lee K. Gleason wrote:
>   I'm (still) looking for a DEC PDT-11/130, a PDP-11 in a VT100 terminal,
> with a pair of TU58 tape drives, to add to the computer museum that I
> call a house. I'd be willing to pay upwards of $200 plus shipping to
> get one in good shape - any software for it would be great too...
> 
>   Also would appreciate any info on RT2, the variant of RT-11 that
> DEC used on these systems.

Lee,

Though I can't offer you the PDT-11/130 that I once owned, I can tell
you about two other units:  a VT103 (truly a PDP-11 in a VT100 case)
and a different PDT (whose model # I can't remember).

I never did get the VT103 working, but it was essentially what you
descrive above as a "PDP-11 in a VT100 terminal."  Where the VT100
motherboard and graphics options cards normally sit, there was a
4x4 QBUS backplane.  Right under the monitor were the 2 TU58 drives.
I believe I even had the boot tape for that machine...but due to a
bad CRT, I never saw it working.  I am quite sure it was called
the VT103, as I still have the instruction manual for it.  I *may*
still be able to locate the unit...it's probably at Yale somwhere.

As for the PDT I actually did get working, it was a large boxy unit
that had 2 8" drives and a small wedge-shaped piece of plastic on
top with 4 LEDs.  Under the wedge-shaped plastic were the 2 PDT-11
motherboards.  I managed to get a hold of a DEC service person
(NOTE: without paying service fees, without having a service
contract, JUST because he was feeling nice one day on his lunch
hour!!) who helped me debug the unit -- and even sent me new
EEPROMS, which finally got it working.  It booted standard RT-11SJ,
and I barely got it working before I accidentally formatted the
RT-11SJ boot disk I had been using. :/

I ended up giving the whole unit (+ offboard VT102) to a friend.
It's possible it's still in his basement, but I can't even 
remember his name.  This was, after all, back in high school :)

Good luck to you, and I hope this helps you in your quest!

-Joan (who STILL needs to get her /34a and 2 11-23s working...)
