In article <351460D6.C20A915@cerritos.edu>, lakha001@cerritos.edu writes:
:Can someone help me out with this.

  It would help if you listed the operating system -- based on the
  error messages, this system is clearly running OpenVMS -- and the
  version of OpenVMS and (in this case) the C compiler.  And for
  OpenVMS questions, posting in comp.os.vms is a better choice.

: I am trying to write a simple C
:program on DEC/Alpha system. For some reason, it just won't compile. I
:am not sure what I am missing out. I looked at the HELP pages on the
:system and the CC run time routines do show popen as a command.

  With DEC C, one often sees the use of the /PREFIX=ALL qualifier,
  to force the routines to be prefixed for the LINK.  (There is some
  magic going on here, for now it is best to simply add "/PREFIX=ALL"
  to the DEC C compiler incantation.  It's rather easier to explain
  this as "magic", by the way. :-)

  Also note that the popen() and similar calls were added in the DEC C
  run-time support in OpenVMS V7.0 and later.  To use these calls on
  versions of OpenVMS prior to V7.0, one needs to avail ones self of the
  DEC C V5.6 and later "backport" library, as described in the DEC C
  documentation set.  (To see the current OpenVMS version, look at the
  first line of the output from a SHOW SYSTEM command.)

  There exists callable mail interface in OpenVMS, see the OpenVMS
  Utility Routines Reference Manual for details.

  Prior to this release, I would expect one would need to use a single
  line such as the following:

    system("MAIL filespec user/subject=\"subjecttext\" " );

  I'm not sure that PMDF will work this way, but MAIL does...

  Or one could use mailboxes, and pass the commands into the
  subprocess one at a time, specifying the mailbox as the input
  device for a lib$spawn() command, or via a system() call with
  the command specification of "@mbxdevname:".  Remember to send
  along a ^Z (EOF, <CTRL/Z>) or EXIT as the last command...

  Or one could use a text file, and execute it via @filespec,
  via a call to lib$spawn() or via system().

:Please respond directly by mailing me back since I dont frequent the
:newsgroups.

  Ask here, get an answer here.

