I recently ported a load of our software over to AlphaNT, and it was very
simple.

All our development is done using VC++ 5.0, and all that was required was to
copy all of the development area over to the Alpha, create the new
configurations under the Alpha native version of Visual Studio (ie: as well
as the standard Debug & Release builds, you add AXPDebug and AXPRelease) and
recompile and do your testing. If you subscribe to MSDN (not sure what level
is required) you probably have all native Alpha software you need.

If you can, avoid using FX!32 as there are some limitations to what it can
do, and it can complicate the installation process a bit. The support for
things like ODBC are currently limited, although a new version 1.3 should be
available soon.

Assuming you have the compilers and the like, you can pick up a cheap AXP
machine easily these days, new machines can be bought for a few thousand
(around the same price as decent intel PC's), and old (slow but usable for
porting) machines can be found second hand for hundreds.

Here in the UK we got an old AXPpc 150, the other week fo �500, with disks,
and memory, we had to get keyboard, mouse and monitor, but they are just
standard PC bits. These machines may not be that fast but they are very
usfull. These machines also suport OpenVMS and Unix as well as NT.

The biggest expense in all of this is getting the Alpha support in
InstallSheild, and this was also the main time consuming bit of the process.

Hope this is of some help,

Laurence Blunt


