You most probably have a problem with a failing interconnect lockout, ie;
one of the small microswitches on one of the covers.

For troubleshooting purposes, there is, inside the power system box, a
small connector interconnected with the cover microswitches. You can place
a small jumper in this and see if the unit powers up. If so, then the
problem is with a microswitch. Probably a screw is loose and only a small
adjustment and re-tightening is required.

As the warranty on the system is three years, why is DIGITAL not fixing
your problem?

The only other interlock I am aware of is through the tachometer in the
three cooling fans. If a fan were failing, then that would power down the
system.

The connectors on the fans in some units had the wiring not crimped
properly. Most if not all of these problems were caught in manufacturing,
but I suppose it's possible a bad wiring harness could have got to the
field.

WARNING! the jumper I mentioned above is strictly for testing /
troublshooting and running with any of the covers of the system unit open
will result in overheating and damage to the system unit, violation of all
RFI and EMI rules and regulations.

