>Tim Shoppa wrote:
>In article <01bd4840$9bd55c40$0c01a8c0@ws2.tse.com>,
>Jay <jlwest@tseinc.com> wrote:
>>System is a PDP11/73 running RT11 5.04.
>>Supposedly both the RQDX2 and RQDX3 controllers support 4 drives.
>>Some documentation specifically says 4 hard drives. Is this in
>>addition to an RX50? So - can either the RQDX2 or RQDX3 support
>>four hard drives AND an RX50 (under RT11 5.04 of course)?
>The limit on a RQDXn is 4 physical drives.  A RX50 counts as two drives
>if you want to use both halves.  It is possible to configure things
>so that you only use half the RX50 and have three hard drives.

Jerome Fine replies:

I have been tied up with some personal matters, so I did not get a
chance to add to what Tim Shoppa has stated - all of which is
correct.

Also, I note that Megan Gentry recently supplied these posts
with the information as to how to have three hard drives and half
of the RX50 when using a BA123 box.

                                                                  Largest Supported
Controller                                                        Hard Drive
RQDX1  (M8039)       (Must be last module)    RD51
RQDX1  (M8039-YA)(Must be last module)    RD52
RQDX2  (M8039-YB)                                     RD53
RQDX3  (M7555)                                            RD54


>> If not,
>>does RT11 5.04 support multiple RQDXx controllers ok?
>Of course.  Just make a second copy of your DU handler (COPY DU.SYS DA.SYS),
>and SET DA:CSR=1nnnnn, SET DA:VEC=yyy, where 1nnnnn is the CSR
>of the second RQDX and yyy is an unused vector on your system.
>Reboot and the devices on the second controller will be accessible as
>DA0:, etc.
>Alternatively, do a SYSGEN to create the second MSCP handler.
>Or - even better - upgrade to RT-11 V5.5 or later and use the new
>DU handler that's capable of up to 64 logical units and up to 4 controllers.

RT-11 V5.4G supports multiple controllers within ONE device driver,
but it must be SYSGENed.  However, as Tim Shoppa noted, only
8 "logical" devices are supported in the "mapping".  In addition,
there are 3 restrictions:
(a)  If you make a version (COPY  DU.SYS  DA.SYS) and do the
      "SET  DA  CSR=",   "SET  DA  VECTOR=", be sure to
      do the sets immediately after the copy since (although it should
      not matter) you do not want to have both DU and DA with the
      same CSRs and VECTORs when you boot.
(b) You will not be able to "BOOT/FOREIGN  DAn:" since
      the name of the device driver must be in the boot block of the
      device driver and via a COPY, the boot code in DA.SYS will
      have the old DU name in the boot block  -  which means that
      a hardware boot also cannot occur.  However, if you do a
      SYSGEN for DA.SYS correctly (you need to change up
      to about 6 values and symbols), then DA.SYS will be OK.
(c)  There is one instruction (plus other supporting instructions)
       missing in the boot code for V5.4G of DU.SYS which
       prevents booting non-zero partitions of hard disk drives -
       which I understand Jay does not have (only RD52s).
       This problem has been fixed for V5.4G via a privately
       available patch which I presume Mentec would not want
       posted in this forum without the usual disclaimers that:
       (1)  The patch is not supported by Mentec
       (2)  The patch is not authorized by Mentec
       (3)  The patch is to be used at the risk of the user and
              is supplied without any charge or warranty of any
              kind to only hobby users.
       (4)  There are many other bugs in V5.4G which must
              also be fixed and fixing this one bug in the device
              driver does not fix any of the other bugs.
       (5)  In particular,  none of the Y2K issues in V5.4G
              would be fixed by fixing this bug in the DU.SYS
              device driver.
       Note that V5.6 does have this problem fixed for the
       first 8 "logical" drives since the boot block in V5.6 does
       allow non-zero partitions.  However, if there are more
       partitions (a SYSGEN in V5.6 can enable extended
       device drivers),  the mapping table in the boot block
       is only 8 entries long and only the first 8 logicals can
       be booted.  This problem has been fixed for V5.6 via
       a privately available patch which would have to include
       the same disclaimers as above plus the restriction that
       the privately available patch allows for only one
       hardware controller when 64 partitions are all bootable
       (a version which allows all 4 possible controllers for
       up to perhaps 32 partitions could be written, but was
       not deemed sufficiently worthwhile).

>>Also, are the RD53 drives supported as above (ie with both/either
>>RQDX2 or RQDX3)?
>Yes.  Keep in mind that the low-level-format for hard drives is different
>for the RQDX2 and RQDX3, and moving a drive between controllers will
>require that you reformat using XXDP+.

I have found no exceptions to this situation for the RQDX2 and RQDX3.

I have found that in practice, the format for the RQDX1 and RQDX2 also
seem to be different almost all of the time.  However, I seem to have
encountered an RQDX1 (M8039-YA) in my travels which formats an
RD52 in a manner that is recognized by the RQDX2 (M8039-YB).
By accident, the RQDX1 was used and the format done with that particular
RQDX1 (for the RD51s and RD52s) was acceptable to the RQDX2
to which those RD51s and RD52s were attached.  I no longer use RD5ns
on a regular basis, but I think that the RQDX1 in question is still available
from a friend who might have kept it for some illogical reason (a pack rat?).

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict




