In article <6eud85$p4f$2@jetsam.uits.indiana.edu>, bdwheele@indiana.edu 
says...
> Would I see a speed/reliability increase if I switched from an RA82 to
> an RA7x disk?  I hate to buy a disk for this machine (especially since the
> disk would cost more than I paid for this box), but if It'd make the box
> faster, I might do it.  I get a fault light every now and then, but no
> errors are getting reported...
> 
> Also, what _are_ the RA72?  I can't find too much info on them, but they
> appear to be modern disks with an SDI interface.  Are they the same size
> as the RA82?  What cabling would I need if I were to hook it up at the same
> time as my RA82?  
> 
> Thanks!
> Brian Wheeler
> bdwheele@indiana.edu
> 

If been going through this myself for the last several days, trying to 
get rid of a bunch of clunky old RA81's.

There is no question the RA72's would be much much much more reliable 
than RA82's.  They will also be much faster (2-3x access time), and
use much less power.

The problem is mounting them.  They are 5 1/4 inch, full-height drives
(microscopic compared to 19 inch RA8x's), and live in a box called an
SABB (I think).  This is a box that occupies 1/2 the space of an RA82.
(You can put 2 of them side-by-side in the space occupied by an RA82.)
Each box can hold 4 RA7x drives.  There is a series of special cabinets
pre-wired to hold SABB's, with cables pre-installed to connect each
SABB box to 8 SDI connectors at the back (each drive in the SABB can be
dual ported, so you need 8 cables for the 4 drives.)  The SDI connectors
are identical to those on the RA82's and RA72's use the same controllers.
To VMS, they look just like RA82's, only bigger and faster.

Here's the rub:  RA72's seem to be plentiful and relatively cheap, 
judging by used equipment ads.  However, no one seems to list the SABB's
(SA7x's, I think is the part number), or the cabinets preconfigured to
hold them (SA600 or SA800, IIRC).  The couple of vendors we called didn't
have any in stock.

I seem to recall from a post long ago that it is possible to mount the 
SA7x SABB in an RA82-type cabinet (or anywhere there is a free 19 inch
rack space, 10 1/2 inches tall), and use random SDI cables and bulkheads,
scavenged from RA8x's, to hook them up.  (Caution: this might only apply
to RA9x's, not RA7x's.)  I think there is some kind of cross-over in the 
cables or in the bulkheads, so you need an odd number of cables between 
the controller and the drive.  Generally there are 3, controller to 
system bulkhead, external (heavy black rectangular cable) to drive
bulkhead, and internal drive cable from drive bulkhead to drive.
You can't just take the (long) external cable off your RA82 and plug it
directly into the SA7x; you need to swap the signals by using the fan-out
cable and a set of bulkheads, or by faking it with an extra cable and
bulkhead.  (The bulkheads are the little square boxes, about 3 inches
square and one inch thick, with a pair of SDI cable sockets on the top
and bottom flat square surfaces.  They usually are gold-colored, and have
"A" and "B" scratched on the side to indicate the A and B ports.)

BTW, does anyone know if you can mount a raw RA72 in the spaces designed
for RA70's in a MicroVAX 3600?  (This is a wide q-bus cabinet with space
at the top for a TK70 tape drive, two RA70's and an SDI distribution
panel, so you can hook up external SDI drives to the other 2 ports on the
system's KDA50.)  We have such a beast, with a dead RA70, and are 
wondering if we could put a bigger, fast drive in there instead.

John Santos



