D.Webb wrote:
> 
> ...
>
> True this is under programming features and specifically say
> Decwindows -
> but can you actually run Decwindows without motif anymore ?
> 
> However when I read further p 4-3
> 
> Note that the version of the X Window System compatible X programming
> library
> (Xlib) currently shipped with DECwindows Motif Version 1.2-4 for
> OpenVMS does
> not support these new extensions.
> 


  The X11 design has a bright line drawn between the client bits -
  which include the application, Xlib, Motif, Toolkits, etc. - and
  the server and its extensions.  The bright line is the wire
  protocol.

  The two parts really are distinct, and in Digital they are done
  by multiple groups, with different schedule requirements.  The
  "client" bits are bundled together in the "Motif" layered product,
  and the "server" bits are bundled with OpenVMS as the DECwindows
  saveset.

  The V7.1 server is based on the X11R6 sample implementation.
  And because of the wire protocol, it is compatable with clients
  using *any* revision of the MIT sample release.  Remember - its X
  Windows Version 11.  The protocol version remains the same, and
  *extensions* are just that - optional extensions to the protocol
  that the client must determine if it exists before it can use them.

  Unfortunately, about the time that the V7.1 server was done, the
  status of Motif for VMS was changed to maintenance only... so it is
  not likely that it will ever be upgraded to use the X11R6 sample
  features.

  As to "can you use X11 without Motif"  the answer is you "should"
  be able to, but some of the files used by the server (like the
  commmand files to configure and start it) are supplied in the
  Motif kit (when the Motif LP was created, the split of the files
  was not as clean as it might have been).

  You certainly can hack the command files once Motif has been
  installed, so that only the X11 server is started (I do it all
  the time for debug) -- and connect to the workstation from
  another client - say a current UNIX server system.

