Adam Stouffer <tesla@NO-SPAM.sgi.net> wrote:
: Can anyone tell me some info about a DECstation 3100? Can I use a normal
: PC monitor on it? What type of ram does it use? What software does it
: run? Thanks

The DECstation 3100 is a workstation based on a MIPS R2000A CPU running
at 16 MHz. It includes a (non-fast, non-wide) SCSI port, 10 MBit ethernet
with AUI and 10Base2-connectors and a grahpics controller that can
display 1024x864 pixel with 1 and 8 bit color depth, depending on the
type of video RAM SIMM that is installed. It's not a fast machine by
today's standards but can still be very useful.

You will need a monitor that accepts sync on green. Otherwise, most of
the 17" monitors available will work. The screen resolution of the 
DECstation is fixed at 1024x768, you can find exact timing specs and
lots more interesting info (if you're an operating system hacker type, at
least :-)) in ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/DEC/specs/DS3100.ps.Z

The RAMs are DEC-proprietary 80-pin ECC SIMMs with 2 MB capacity per SIMM.
You can put 24 MB maximum into the 3100.

The native operating system for the DECstations is Ultrix/MIPS. The latest
release is 4.5. You can also run NetBSD and OpenBSD (see http://www.netbsd.org
and http://www.openbsd.org) on the 3100 and we're still working on the Linux
port (http://decstation.unix-ag.org).

regards,
