>NT?  Ease of maintainence???  Huh?  You certainly can't be talking about
>that product from Microsoft, can you? 

Eh?  MS must've produced two NTs - the one you got and the one everyone
else got.  The reason why I'm moving all the stuff I can onto NT now is
BECAUSE of its ease of maintenance, not in spite of it.


> Not to mention that, as a general statement, "NT performs worse than Unix
> on the same hardware" appears to be pretty much true.

So what?  Speed is not the only yardstick by which we choose OS for our
systems.  If that were the case, we'd have never moved from DOS to
Win3.11 and higher on our PCs.  Interopability, usability and support
are all key issues in a production environment.  Certainly an NT server
will be better at all three of these in his environment as it will fit
in rather nicely with the NT4 workstations on his network.

The only question wrt to performance here is whether or not NT on the
chosen hardware platform will meet his desired performance requirements,
if he's defined any.  

