> > just returning from Hannover (CeBIT). At the digital Area in Hall 1,
> > located in the HPC area, I met a very happily smiling VMSler. Reason:
> > digital was showing the first Galaxies system.
> > Whow: Running on a Turbolaser with 12 CPUs, lots of memories, running 2
> > VMS-systems nicely sharing CPUs and memory.
> 
>         Actually, this, or a similarly configured system, was running at
>     DECUS Anaheim last November,  configured  with  _3_ nodes (systems),
>     and  moving the allocation of 12 cpus amongst the three.  Yes,  lots
>     of smiles!  :-) :-)

Nope, what was shown at DECUS was the concept of hard-partitioning, or
enabling multiple DISPARATE OSes to co-reside in the same box. There was
also a Galaxy demo, but that was separate and independent of The Three
(OS) Faces of TurboLaser.

You will see this technology again. Picture, if you will, a 32 or 64-CPU
WildFire system running two (or three) different OSes. The ability to
host VMS, WNT, and UNIX concurrently on the same box sure puts a
different light on affinity or connectivity, does it not?

Of course, it gets better. Vendors with big-endian Unix and
little-endian WNT will not be able to run multiple OSes on the same box.
With DUNIX, WNT, OVMS, and Alpha--and in the future, IA64, Bravo, and
WNT, DEC customers will not face the Custer's Last Stand of the Big
Endians.


> 
> >                                             There was no given application,
> > but some basic demonstration software showing the cpu and memory balancing.
> 
>         I'm not sure what you mean by "no given application".  There was
>     a full  hour  session  a  DECUS  by  Steve  Zalewski  explaining the
>     "application"   of  Galaxy  software  to  some  very  real  business
>     situations, and those model business situations were demonstrated in
>     the VMS Campground.
> 

The beauty of OVMS Galaxy is that you don't need "given" applications.
Anything that runs today on OVMS will run just fine on a Galaxy.


>         BTW, I was suitably impressed  that the Galaxy software supports
>     a  Motif  interface  (very snazzy :-), a DCL interface  (so  command
>     procdures can do the work), _and_ a  full  set  of  System  Services
>     interfaces  (so  you  can do the work with calls from a user-written
>     program).
> 

All this and more. The only downside is that the technology will not
ship as a product until near the end of the year. This, I surmise, is
strictly a marketing decision. I further surmise that if sufficient
customer demand existed, field test software could be made available
much earlier.





