Charles M Richmond (cmr@iisc.com) wrote:
: I am sorry that you don't seem to grok the use of "ALT" in the 
: source. In properly designed browsers each clickable spot is 
: thusly labeled and not just by the latest version of lynx. "ALT" 
: is supported by every character only browser.

The issue is not the use of ALT, either in an IMG tag or in an AREA
tag.  The issue is what constitutes a "clickable spot".  The Digital
quash site has almost no clickable spots for browsers which do not
support client-side imagemaps.  Whether the browser is text-only
or graphical or graphical with image loading turned off makes no
difference.  No support for client-side imagemaps?  No access to
the Digital "quash" pages.  The end.

Interestingly, the Digital quash page includes the beginning of
markup for server-side imagemaps in addition to client-side.
The former is characterized by ISMAP attributes in the IMG tags
AS WELL AS a hypertext ref around the whole image.  The latter
is characterized by a USEMAP attribute instead, along with the
MAP and AREA tags to define for the client what area does what.
Several of the document's IMG tags have the ISMAP attribute, but
none but the QUASH graphic itself has a hypertext link, and that
doesn't work.  So, it looks like the author *started* to do the
right thing, but never completed the job.

: To blame the company that produces the web page is a failure to 
: take responsibility and to put out a flame telling people that 
: the page is broken when it is your browser that is bust, is not
: nice.

Come on Charles!  A browser that doesn't support your preferred
featureset is not "broken", just different.  Web architecture
was designed almost from the start to be platform independent
and both forward and backward compatible, assuming competent
web authorship.  The Digital quash page, however, is so badly
broken that it will not validate using *any* DTD on WebTechs.
Come on Digital!  You too can do better.

: PPS DEC gets enough 'flack' for stuff that they really did wrong,
: lets try not to bust their chops when they really did not foul up!

They did foul up by placing pages on the web which have distinct
accessibility problems.  We should inform them of this so that they
can improve in the future.



