:>It seems that my VAX 11/730's R80 is having a problem. Long ago I was able
:>to use Stand Alone Backup to copy itself onto the R80 without any problem.
:>Now I get
:>
:>  %BACKUP-E-READBAD error reading bad block data on DQ1:
:>  -SYSTEM-F-CTRLERR fatal controller error
:>
:>repeated a bunch of times.
:>
:>Does anyone have any idea what's wrong with it? (DQ is the correct device, 
:>right?)
:
:I thought the R80 was always unit zero.  Do you (somehow) have more than
:one unit connected?  I didn't know that more than one R80 was even supported!
:
:(And I think it was DQA0:, but DQ0: ought to be the same thing.)

  VAX/VMS (OpenVMS) version?  BACKUP command?  Device type?

  Most R80 IDC disks I've seen were DQ devices, and were usually refered
  to as the DQA0: device -- a quick trip through the information displayed
  by a "SHOW DEVICE D/FULL" command should tell this for sure, however.

  The other drive commonly found on these systems is the 10MB RL02 disk
  drive -- the RL02 is normally known as device DQA1:, when hooked up via
  the IDC.

  If you have access to the diagnostics, you might try a disk (re)format.
  This will clobber all data, obviously.

  One could build standalone on the RL02, but stuffing a bootable version
  of VMS onto the 10MB RL02 drive took a bit more effort.  (One could
  get the important parts of VAX/VMS V3.x, and enough of DECnet to be
  useful, stuffed into about 4 MB.  But I digress.)  And one would need
  a number of RL02 platters to back up a full R80 (121MB) disk to RL02.

  If this is the RL02 drive involved, I'd strongly recommend against
  swapping any disks around -- until you get this resolved.  If the RL02
  drive has hardware problems, it is possible that it will clobber any
  (still good) disk platter inserted into the drive.

  In any event, this VAX-11/730 is sick, and is due for a visit from your
  local hardware service technician.  The R80 disk and/or the RL02 and/or
  the VAX-11/730 IDC disk controller may or may not be "toast".

	--

  It might be cheaper to replace the whole system with a used VAX system
  (VAXstation, MicroVAX, VAXserver, etc) than it would be to fix this
  VAX-11/730.  This assumes that you don't have any "unique" hardware
  on the UNIBUS, of course, and it assumes that the local software can
  be moved forward to a more recent VMS release.  (The VAX-11/730 was
  first supported by VAX/VMS V3.0 -- I might call the VAX-11/730 system
  an antique, but then I remember buying new ones and installing them. :-)

