In article <350DB3EB.73D817AC@cnct.com>, John DiVizio <johnd@cnct.com> writes:
:...I've had this [DEC 3000 model 600] since 1994 w/o an operating system
:and every once in a while I get the urge to turn it on and get depressed
:whenb I see the cursor just blinking back at me on the screen. I was
:hoping there would be an easier, cost efficient way as to breathing a
:little life into this. But getting back to the ploblem at hand, when
:I was getting responses to my searching for an Open VMS AXP OP/sys...

  The appropriate choice of operating system is specific to application
  availability, and also to local staff choices -- there is no one true
  operating system, much as there is no one true text editor.  Each has
  its strengths, and each has its weaknesses.

  Given your comments, I'd recommend trying to sell the system.  This
  act will cure your power-on depression, and this will also provide a
  cheap cure for your fear of OS commitment. :-)

  OpenVMS Alpha and DIGITAL UNIX can be used on this system, and I'd
  check around and see if there are any TURBOchannel drivers available
  for Linux.  There are probably other operating systems around that
  will run on this platform, too.  (Check the Linux groups for more
  information on Linux support.)

  I would recommend finding out if there were any operating system
  licenses that can be transfered with this system, and then working
  to transfer these licenses to yourself or to your company.

  Once you figure out what license(s) you can transfer, you can better
  decide what operating system to pick.

  There is no DECUS hobbyist license for OpenVMS Alpha.  (Hobbyist
  licenses are available only for OpenVMS VAX V6.1 -- for information
  on these licenses, see http://www.montagar.com/.)

  My preference would be for OpenVMS, but that probably doesn't meet
  your "cheap" requirement -- unless you've got an existing OpenVMS
  license with the box that you can and are willing to pay to transfer.

  For technical details on DEC 3000-class workstations, see the DIGITAL
  Technical Journal, Volume 4 Number 4, the 1992 special edition.

