I believe that the difference is where the actual memory is located on the
AXP chip or in close proximity to it with fast access times.  The Floating
point calculations are what are going to buy you speed with FEA.  also the
Disk access times help somewhat.  REading in large files and then swapping
them out when you run out of RAM .

here are the features for Scientific calculations you want to look for.

High Floating point speed, you can check this with the SPECBENCH web page.
Lotta RAM, as much as you can afford.
Fast disk, stripped RAID 0+1 or something.
FAST network interface FDDI or Full Duplex 100BaseT


What your looking for is the quickest way to get the data from disk or
network to RAM for the CPU to chew on it.  Operating system plays very
little in the equation but UNIX has a better Swapper and can handle
Symetrical Multi-Processing better than NT.  So if your looking at a big
server with 2 or more CPU's look very hard at UNIX vs NT.

Look at the specbench page for spec mark information and then at the TPC
counsel page for over all system performance.

There are a hundred stories out there and it all depends on your specific
business needs as to which one you need to listen to.






