phil@yrl.co.uk (Phil Ottewell) writes...
}  This is an update of the 2.3 VMS Performance Meter, based on an original
}program by Robert Heller, enhanced by Mark Pizzolato, Joe Kazimierczyk,
}Dean Schamberger and myself. The performance meter show various VMS system
}metrics; free memory, disk IO, a graph of CPU usage in each modes, and a
}history graph of total CPU usage.
}   - Phil Ottewell

My guess would be that the general idea for what is shown is based on the old
VWS Banner program by Peter George (from October 1984). This was from the VWS
demos (source and executable located in the [VWSDEMO.B32] directory and .COM
file template to set up logical names for it in [VWSDEMO.COM], if you happen
to have an old VAXstation that runs, or used to run, VWS). This has various
things that can be shown. The one I've got running on a VAXstation 3100/m48
right now shows 4 sections, the left one is an analog clock with the day of
the week, the date, and the time on three lines below it, the one to its right
is a scrolling total CPU time graph, followed by a bar chart of time in
processor modes, and another section with counts and deltas for free pages,
modified pages, page faults, paging I/O, remote I/O, system disk I/O, ethernet
I/O, and user disks I/O - some of these, the ones starting with system
disk I/O, are optional and defined by the user via logical names, and there
are others that can be added or substituted (like a rotating cube and a
message window). The various sections have different update intervals - I
have it set to update the clock every second, the total CPU time update
every 2 seconds, the time in modes update every 3 seconds, and the I/O counts
update very 5 seconds. 

A very spiffy little utility written in about 13KB of Bliss.

--- Carl



