In article <wxlnujhj8q.fsf@earth.ox.ac.uk>, rubber@bounce.com (Keith Refson - real email address in signature) writes:

> You imply that LaTeX is a second-best solution for those on a budget.
> You *can* pay for some versions if you are determined but that's not
> the point.
> 
> Why not use LaTeX because TeX is the best typesetting engine in
> existence, and certainly by far the most portable?

Both DEC Document and LaTeX use TeX for generating output, so preference
between them must be based on some other factor such as what you like
for input style, how you prefer to get support (buying support from
vendor A is _not_ the same answer as buying support from vendor B), etc.

Portability may or may not be a virtue, depending on your own needs.
If you have no need to run on multiple operating systems, portability
is not useful for its own purposes.

Certainly most portable programs are harder for a VMS-centric person
to use because they tend toward not following standard DCL usage,
etc.  Someone from another background who just happens to be on
a VMS system may prefer a portable program just for the command
syntax.

Larry Kilgallen



