The MMJ ports do not have enough lines to support modems. I cannot remember
the exact pin order as it's nee years since I last made up a converter
cable, but there are  only 6 wires, which are Read-Ground, Ready-to-send,
and Receive, these are mirrored left/right with equivelent Transmit lines.

You can connect a modem to one of these ports if you play around and patch
together pins on the 25pin plug for the modem, but the modem will be unable
to handle automatic hangup when you logoff. You or your user would have to
manually hangup from their end of the line.

It has been a very long time since I last looked at a 3100/38 type machine,
but I thought they always had a single 25 pin full modem control port on
them? You other options would include looking around for some secondhand
kit, there was an expantion card which supported 8 or 16 full modem control
lines, or look around for an old terminal server like a 200MC (must be the
MC which indicates the modem control lines are used).

