Asuming that this is a Multia (as indicated below), this would definately
not be a good machine for running any kind of serious cad software on. The
machines were designed (years ago) as replacements for dumb terminals more
than anything else, even though most seem to be running NT well enough. But
the performance of these machines is less than an average Intel Pentium
machine of today.

Don't get confused about it being an Alpha, and being fast. In there day
these were realy fast machines, but things have moved on a lot. The average
Alpha bought today, are probably 533Mhz machines with the second generation
Alpha CPU's in them. They will also have a decent amount of memory, and fast
disk systems.

>
>Personally, I would not do this.  I think what you're talking about is a
>166 mhz Multia or UDB.  These sound fast, but running NT 4 and anything
>non-alpha native is a nitemare.  These systems are decent if you load
>them up with RAM and run Linux on them, but NT 4 is no good.  It runs
>applications in 16 bit mode, which is ridiculous on a 64 bit processor.
>You'd be much better off getting an x86 machine to run NT.


With regards to the above:

You know absolutly nothing about running NT on Alpha machines, come on,
admit it.

Alpha NT machines can run 16bit windows and even many old DOS applications,
using pure software emulation. They can also run a large amount of Intel
32bit applications written for W95 and NT on Intel boxes, by using the
software emulation/translation system called FX!32, which is free from
digital (a new version 1.3 should be available soon which has greater
compatibility and features, including support for NT services). Then there
are the Alpha native applications which run at full speed. Quite a lot of
software is being supported natively on this platform, and the number is
still growing.

NT4.0 is a 32bit OS, and for all but a very few systems used in the world
today that is still more than enough. I wonder just how many applications
you run on that Linux box of yours are using more than a few MB's let along
GB's of memory. The NT file system (NTFS not the old FAT) is a 64bit file
system so storing huge amounts of data on disk is not a problem for anyone.

Laurence Blunt.






