In article <6du5h7$at$1@newsy.ifm.liu.se>, janha@lysator.liu.se (Jan H�keg�rd) writes:

:Have a SCANPROC 8 PLANE 2D board here (for VAXstation 3100).
:
:Is this the same as SPX graphics?

  Yes, it is.

:It has two DIP switches, but no matter in which of the four combinations
:these are put I can't get a picture on the VR299 monitor.

  The VR299 is compatible with the SPX.

  For fixed-frequency monitor information, see the website
  http://www.paranoia.com/~filipg/HTML/REPAIR/F_fix_freq.html

  (And no, I don't know the meanings for the switchpacks on the SPX.)

:What resolutions do the SCANPROC support?

  SPX (1280 x 1024 x 66 Hz) and GPX 1024 x 864 x 60 Hz.

:Is it better or worse than (or is it the same board as) SPX?

  Yes.

:Does it have a very long initialization (compared to GPX) which
:means I just didn't wait long enough for a picture? GPX quite
:quickly starts to display test pictures in the same machine,
:while this board just flickered the screen.

  Both the SPX and the GPX run through some self-test displays.  I've
  not noticed a big difference in the graphics self-test operations.
  (Most of the time spent in a bootstrap seems more dependent on the
  speed of the VAX or Alpha processor, on the system disk I/O, and on
  the contents of the system startup.  Not on the graphics self-test.
  The self-test overhead tends to get lost in the "noise".)

