Patrick507 wrote:
> 
> I have the oportunity to pick up a VX40B-F2  133mhz cheap and was thinking
> about a running NT 4.0 and using it for a CAD station as well as running
> general PC software.
> 
> Is it a good idea???
> 
> Also, the system is without memory,  the only "True" parity SIMMs i've found
> are 4meg.  Can regular parity SIMMS be used??
> 
> Thanks for any help
> 
> Pat

Personally, I would not do this.  I think what you're talking about is a
166 mhz Multia or UDB.  These sound fast, but running NT 4 and anything
non-alpha native is a nitemare.  These systems are decent if you load
them up with RAM and run Linux on them, but NT 4 is no good.  It runs
applications in 16 bit mode, which is ridiculous on a 64 bit processor. 
You'd be much better off getting an x86 machine to run NT. 

As far as the RAM goes, you have to use "True" parity SIMMS rather than
"logic" parity SIMMS.  That's the difference.  "True" parity simms can
be found in sizes greater than 4mb, you just have to look carefully.
Hope this helps
-m. howell



