> > These are terminal servers with 8 RS-232 ports and one AUI ethernet port.
> 
> Right.
> 
> > The only protocol the 200/MC understands is LAT,
> > you can't use it with standard TCP/IP telnet
> 
> If I want to use one terminal to connect to several different machines,
> or allow several terminals to connect to one machine, I guess I could
> use the DECserver for that purpose.

We are still running a couple of DS200/MC. We've used them in the past
for both the above
activities. Nowadays I use them mainly to hang serial printers and our
DSN connection off.

> 
> Can I have a cluster with one machine connected to the outside world
> through ethernet with standard TCP/IP and use the terminal server to
> connect to (at least) the (other) machines in the cluster?

I'm not clear what you are driving at here. You can run TCP/IP and LAT
at the same time
on your cluster, so you can connect to any node on the clauter from the
DS200 via LAT,
and then telnet elsewhere if you wish.

> 
> > You
> > will also need a bootimage for it which is booted via the MOP protocol.
> 
> Where should the boot image reside and where can I get one?
> 

The DS200 software is on the standard CD layered product distribution
(both VAX and ALPHA).
The boot image is called PR0801ENG.SYS and normally lives in
SYS$COMMON:[DECSERVER], which
should be added to MOM$LOAD (DECnet Phase IV) or MOP$NAMED_LOAD
(DECnet/OSI).

