Content-Type: text/plain; charset=US-ASCII
Organization: Personal Opinions Only
Mime-Version: 1.0
Reply-To: lederman@eisner.decus.DISABLE-JUNK-EMAIL.org
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



I don't have a bare RZ55 drive handy, but I have used them
on PCs in external cabinets, and it was necessary to use
an external terminator.  The information I have available
indicates that there is no on-board SCSI terminator that
can be switched in.  I have looked at another drive
in the series (RZ57) and there are no sockets on the drive
to plug in terminating resistors: though there might be
on the older RZ55.

If you really can fit this inside the PC cabinet, it may
be sufficient to turn on the termination built into the
SCSI controller itself.  If not you may have to add a
terminator inside, or on the external connector, or use
one of those 'loop around' SCSI cables that effectively
put both ends of the cable at the controller, where it is
terminated.  Also, check the SCSI controllers on-board
BIOS or other settings (if it has any) to see what it
says about termination.

My experience with PC SCSI is that the only way to know
for certain if something will work is to try it.

-- 
