In article <1998Mar10.001032.17297@decus>,
PDP-11 Trainee Guru <geremin@decus.org.au> wrote:
>	I did some fiddling on a client site today with an 11/94.
>
>	Unfortunately, I did not have the NEWS posts with me so I had
>	forgotten that 1-MAR-2000 was a problem date. I did not find
>	any real problems, but I may not have been thorough enough.
>	I only had a couple of hours to play with it.
>...
>   2.	On power up (after self tests) it only displays a 2 digit year.
>	So how do we really know that the TOY clock is doing '2000' or 
>	'1900'. When I typed in '01-JAN-00' it showed 'Sat' - so I assumed
>	it was '2000' because 31-DEC-99 showed 'Fri'. 

The TOY chip used in the 11/93 and 11/94 is the Dallas Semiconductors
DS1215, which only stores two decimal digits for the year.  The RT-11
5.6/5.7 TOY routines interpret the digits from "80" to "99" as being in
the 1900's and digits in the range "00" to "79" as being in the 2000's
- I would guess most OS's have chosen to do the same.

As you've discovered, there's no problem with the 31-DEC-1999 to
01-JAN-2000 rollover.

>   4.	I did set the date at 28-Feb-00  and saw it rollover to 29-Feb-00.

Yep, this works fine too.  The real problem is the 29-Feb-2000 to
1-Mar-2000 rollover.

>   Q.	My real question arising from my tests, etc is -
>	What is the life of the Lithium Battery on the CPU module ?
>	One module I know has only had intermittent use in the past 2
>	years after sitting on the shelf for 6 years. 
>	At least the Maintenance Guide says that it is only used when 
>	the mains power is off.  Has anyone had to change one yet ?

I haven't had to change one.  Looking at the DS1215 data sheet and
my Panasonic battery handbook, it looks like lifetimes of 5-10 years
are to be expected.

I have changed batteries in my Codar Q-bus clock - typical lifetime
there is about 3 years per set of batteries.

>   Q.	What are the chances of there being different versions of the
>	CPU Boot code/TOY code (these are pretty new systems) ?

Every "original" version I've tried has the same version number and date
stamp as yours.

Tim.



