David Hargreaves wrote:
> 
> I've tried using the command
> 
> dump -0f - / | ( cd /mnt; restore -xf - )
> 
> to dump my root filesystem to a new partition mounted on /mnt. The
> command keeps telling me that the destination tape(?) is of zero length.
> What am I doing wrong?

Syntax looks good to me. I don't have ufs file systems or a 
spare disk partion to make one to check your command.
	You should note that dump only works for UFS
not for ADVFS file systems. I suggest that you use vdump/vrestore 
on Digital UNIX for a number of reasons :-

	1/	Works

	2/	Will work for either UFS or ADVFS

	3/	You can use a -D option to specify src and/or dest

eg.
	vdump -0 -f - -D ./tmp/t | vrestore -D /tmp/t2 -xf -

	which you will not two more advances over dump The path
	can be relative ie ./tmp/t can be a directory rather 
	than a file system mount point and does not need to be in 
	/etc/fstab 

	4/	Can handle anything ADVFS can

