> In article <01bd3c66$7db6baa0$6214a6c3@turlington.unidec.co.uk>,
> David Turlington <turlington@unidec.co.uk> wrote:

>>We would like to hear from anyone who is running PDP 11/93-94 systems
>>(Dec CPU's only) particulary in real time environments as the Y2K problem
>>exists only if system is powerfailed after 1st-Mar-2000 it resumes with
>>date 1st-Jan-1900 (also a day of week problem exists)

	OR is it  1-jan-90  ??
> 
> This is a problem with the console firmware on the 11/93-94's.  The
> day of week problem - as far as I know - is purely cosmetic, as no operating
> system actually uses the day of week register on the time-of-year chip.
> But as you note, the rollover problem associated with 29-Feb-2000 to
> 1-Mar-2000 is well known (even mentioned on comp.risks a couple of
> weeks ago!).  I've offered to make a patch to the console firmware
> available, but the folks at both Mentec and Digital say it is *not*
> possible for this to be distributed due to strict non-disclosure
> agreements with ROI.
> 
> Certainly, one workaround is to not use the 11/9x on-board-TOY but instead
> use a separate board, such as one of the CODAR's, that won't get hit
> by the console firmware during reboot from powerfail.  It was a month or so
> ago that I posted patches to the routines supplied by Codar for reading
> and writing the TOY clock, and I've tested these patches under RT-11
> 5.7 and verified that they work.  They should also work under the latest
> RSX-11M+, but I haven't actually tested this.
> 
> Tim.
> 
-- 
	Hi All, especially Tim and Stuart,

	I did some fiddling on a client site today with an 11/94.

	Unfortunately, I did not have the NEWS posts with me so I had
	forgotten that 1-MAR-2000 was a problem date. I did not find
	any real problems, but I may not have been thorough enough.
	I only had a couple of hours to play with it.

	My first test was to set the date back to 1996; just in case.

   1.	Version on KDJ11-E says 'Monitor Version 1.06 30-Jul-1990'
	This system boots up with the full screen display, for the
	user to select a boot device, etc.

   2.	On power up (after self tests) it only displays a 2 digit year.
	So how do we really know that the TOY clock is doing '2000' or 
	'1900'. When I typed in '01-JAN-00' it showed 'Sat' - so I assumed
	it was '2000' because 31-DEC-99 showed 'Fri'. 

   3.	I had some finger trouble at one stage and had it locked up
	with an 'Address Error' flashing, but no KB responsde, Power
	Off, Power On - displays default date of '01-Jan-90 Thu', so
	how do we get any confusion with 1900 ?

   4.	I did set the date at 28-Feb-00  and saw it rollover to 29-Feb-00.

	My scribble notes also say that I could not enter 29-FEB-00, but
	I suspect that it may have been finger trouble. A later note says
	that I did enter 29-FEB-00 but that when I tried to 'save' the
	setting it reverted to 01-JAN-90, once again it may have been
	finger problems - eg me not tabbing properly through the menus, 
	etc. 

   5.	I did set the date for '31-DEC-00' and powered of and let it roll
	over - it powered on with '01-JAN-01 Mon' which sounds OK to me.
	
   Q.	My real question arising from my tests, etc is -
	What is the life of the Lithium Battery on the CPU module ?
	One module I know has only had intermittent use in the past 2
	years after sitting on the shelf for 6 years. 
	At least the Maintenance Guide says that it is only used when 
	the mains power is off.  Has anyone had to change one yet ?

   Q.	What are the chances of there being different versions of the
	CPU Boot code/TOY code (these are pretty new systems) ?

	Incidentally the RSX-11M+ v4.4 at this site still asks for the
	time and date to be entered when starting - and it too only
	displays 2 digits for the year. (and NO, they dont need an
	upgrade - the disks are all WRITE-PROTECTED.)


Regards,   John G.         (aka 'megaJOHN')  ..
