In article <34FF8935.89B36D87@sgi.com> Gints Klimanis <gints@sgi.com> writes:
   Scott Hess wrote:
   > Indeed, that time probably _should_ be ignored, because that
   > extra

   No, it shouldn't be ignored.  Would you propose starting school at
   age 25, when the student is mentally mature and disciplined to make
   the most of a curriculum?

I wasn't referring to college.  I was referring to the years of
pre-college "time spent around computers" that the previous poster was
talking about.

   > time very well may be to their detriment.  Much of the time I
   > consider most productive in my formative years as a programmer
   > was while I didn't even have access to a computer.  Instead, I
   > wrote code - and then reviewed the code, and then reviewed the
   > code, and then reviewed the code.  By the time I _did_ have
   > access to a computer (usually

   You had a wierd computing experience that worked for you.  

I have a computing experience shared with most of the people who've
written today's software.  We're talking about the experience of
people who will be writing tomorrow's software.

   > Too many of the people I recall from college and college age
   > people I've met recently think that lists and trees are things
   > provided by the operating system, and have absolutely no
   > comprehension of the work that someone has put in to provide the
   > components they so blithely put together using a UI builder.
   > They think that things just happen.

   Should anyone be concerned with how to make a UI widget?

Somebody has to.

   Is there any value in compounded techonological development?  Do
   you need to know how a cathode ray tube works to understand the
   computer?  Do you need to understand how to make paper to read a
   book?

You need to understand how a cathode ray tube works to build a
monitor.  You need to have a basic understanding of monitors to build
a computer.  You need to have a basic understanding of paper to
publish a book.

I'll grant that experience with computers during your formative years
will certainly help you _use_ computers.  I'm arguing that it won't
help you much in building the future of computing.

   When your mind is filled with too much supplementary material,
   where's the room for the significant thought?

The human mind is almost inifinitely elastic.  If you aren't using a
notion, it's going to be filed away and won't bother you while you're
working on something else.  While writing this news posting, my
knowledge of how to write UI widgets, and device drivers, and assembly
language isn't interfering with my ability to type in the least.

   > Beyond all that, though, nothing gives you a better appreciation
   > for a fine piece of software than a deep understanding of what
   > you would have to do to implement that software yourself.  And
   > nothing is more annoying than seeing a badly done piece of
   > software when you understand the work necessary to implement it
   > well enough to know that it didn't have to be that way,

   Agreed.  You entire discourse seems to have discounted the
   computing experience of just about every programmer alive.  Yet
   somehow, lots of great software products exist.

There is far more mediocre software than there is great software, in
turn there is far more truly abysmal software than there is mediocre
software.  I'm not saying there are _no_ great programmers out there.
I'm saying that programmers are not made by virtue of mere time spent
around computers - rather, great programmers spend time around
computers because computing strikes a chord with them.

I fell in love with the first computer I saw (an Apple II - not, //e,
not II plus, a II :-), and immediately decided I wanted to program it.
In terms of future programming ability, I'll put that up against
someone who wants a computer merely to play Quake any day of the week.

Later,
