Scott Pope wrote in message ...
>Where exactly are performance increases realized with a larger cache.  The
>new Alpha NT machines are being advertised with a 4 meg Level III cache.


It is very difficult to give a precise percentage of the performance
increase of a chip with
a variable amount of cache: performance is linked to the OS, the
application, the way the application
was compiled and the compiler which the application was created with.

>Is the integer and floating calculation going to be higher?

As a general rule, overall performance is higher with a larger cache.

You can check out http://www.specbench.org where you can find SpecInt and
SpecFp.
if you look at the details of the test, you may find different results
depending on the cache size of the
CPU.

> Is there any
>particular routines (e.g. OpenGL) that benefit a great deal from increased
>cache.

Every routines which needs fast access to memory will benefit from a larger
cache as the data
will be in the cache and not in the much slower main memory.
As far as Alpha are concerned, CPU caches tend to be large in order to feed
the CPU fast enough
due to the blazzing speed of Alphas compared to the rest of the system.

For OpenGL, the performance gain with a larger cache will not be as good as
basic number crunching.
OpenGL performance is much more dependant on the graphic subsystem of the
computer and of the
implementation of OpenGL by the OS.
If you want good OpenGL performance, you would be better with an accelerated
OpenGL video adapter.

For example, I have tested an OpenGL on two x86 NT workstation.
* A Pentium Pro 200 Mhz with 256 kb L2 cache with an accelerated OpenGL
board.
* A Pentium II 266 Mhz with 512 Kb L2 cache with a non-accelerated OpenGL
board.

The second system may be faster for CPU performance, the first one
outperforms by a large margin
the second one for OpenGL stuff.

>What exactly is the difference between Level II and Level III cache?

On Alphas, Level II is integrated on the chip and has a fixed size whereas
Level III is on the motherboard.

As L2 is integrated on the chip, it is much faster than L3.

