vhalkka@cc.helsinki.fi wrote:

> ...
> And you all forgot PDP 9.. I'd say the order is 9-15-11, never seen
> a 12 or a 14.
> ...

The PDP-12 was the successor to the LINC-8; it could execute both PDP-8 and
LINC instructions.  Each instruction set had an instruction to switch over to
the other instruction set starting with the next instruction.

The PDP-14 was an industrial controller with a 1-bit data path.

The PDP-15 did use TTL, but it's bigger than a PDP-11 partly because of lower
packaging density due to smaller modules.  The XVM was a PDP-15/m (XV: Roman
numeral for 15) with a small PDP-11 (11/10?) for an I/O processor.

The PDP-16/m was an off-the-shelf configuration of the PDP-16 modules, which
in turn were descended from the Register Transfer Modules designed at CMU.
(You wired up the data path from data modules (like ALU and registers) and the
flow chart from control modules (like "Kevoke"), and connected the control
lines between the two, to make a custom machine.  Similar philosophy of
hardware design showed up in the Macro-Modules from Washington University in
St. Lous, and the Universal Control Module from the U. of Delaware, which was
a single, flexible control module that was used with a custom TTL data path.)

- Aron Insinga





