Your attitude is somewhat unattractive here, especially since you're wrong.
There is a compiler flag to disable floating-point exceptions of this type;
catching signals is a slow and unnecessarily complex solution to the
problem.

In most scientific contexts, dividing by zero is not only legal but a very
nice idiom. Working with infinity is not particularly difficult.

In fact, IEEE floating-point standards allow it. What the poster does not
know is that there is a compiler flag in DEC C++ that allows division by
zero and other floating-point exceptions to go unreported. It is on by
default because, admittedly, many people such as yourself and others _don't_
intend to divide by zero---so those of us that do should know enough to
enable it. I _disagree_ with DEC's choice here, but so be it.

Michael

