>
>2)  Is there a way in either NT or Linux to write directly to the screen
>memory - i.e. Is the video ram mapped to a physical address? (Like Amiga)?
>

The whole point about having an OS is to use the device drivers that are
supported, so that you are not tied down to running the software on a single
hardware configuration.

NT (and presumably Linux) will not let you access devices directly, unless
you intend writing your own device drivers. Also using the drivers supplied
for any given peace of hardware lets that driver use any hardware assistance
that is available. There are however methods of creating images in main
memory and then asking the OS to move that into the display in one logical
operation which can speed things up a lot.

Remember the Amiga is difference type of system, it had pretty much a fixed
video layout, and was never designed for anything other that single user
mode operartion, so taking over the whole video or sound system was fine for
getting the best performance for games.





