>> >Red Hat that does NAT/masquerading :(
>> 
>> Slackware Linux worked fine when I set it up (and was promptly ordered
>> to format over it and install NT  :-( ).
>> 
>> I believe NetBSD and FreeBSD also support masquerading.
>
>OpenBSD shouls support NAT and it works for any alpha that
>has SRM (not ARC) firmware.

There are a couple of misleading statements here for the price of one.

I agree that OpenBSD/alpha "should" support NAT, but I seriously doubt that
the poster has actually tried it.  The OpenBSD NAT code is essentially the
same code that's in NetBSD (since the author of the software both operating
systems use for NAT commits it directly to the NetBSD tree whenever he thinks
he has a stable release, and AFAICT OpenBSD picks it up from there) and should
suffer from the same poorly-understood problem under OpenBSD as under NetBSD
on the Alpha.

OpenBSD/alpha certainly does *not* "work for any alpha that has SRM (not ARC)
firmware".  OpenBSD's Alpha machine-dependent code is essentially lifted
directly from NetBSD, and it does *not* work on several SRM console machines,
such as the Jensen and the AlphaServer 4100.

There is a large and vibrant community of NetBSD/alpha users and developers,
including a number of engineers at <insert today's name for DEC here> and a
number of people at NASA Ames who work on NetBSD -- usually NetBSD/alpha --
nearly full-time.  Though there are certainly plenty of OpenBSD users out
there, I don't believe many of them use the Alpha port.  Perhaps I'm wrong.
In any event, NetBSD is where most of the code comes from, and if NetBSD has
problem X on the alpha, it's pretty safe to begin with the assumption that
OpenBSD likely does as well.

-- 
