Let me elaborate :|

I have nothing against free software, and I use a fair amount of free 
software myself.  A small fraction of that is FSF software.  If it's 
free, and it works, then I use if if it's the best tool for my job (and 
I do of course abide by the FSF stipulations).  If people want to give 
away software that's fine with me.  If people want to give away money 
I'll take it as well.  No problem.

What I don't like and what keeps me from being an advocate of the FSF is
their stance on software which is not free.  Read their literature; they
talk about the intrinsic evil of selling software.  I'm not suggesting
they sell their software; they can do what they want.  However, I see no
reason for the `holier-than-thou' attitude of putting people down who
charge money for software.  Some software packages require a lot of
effort to produce, and I see no reason why the people who do that work
should not charge money for it.  Sure, if you develop some code for 
yourself anyway and make that publicly available out of the goodness of 
your heart and appreciate it when others do so, that's fine.  I do that 
myself.  However, I recognise that not ALL software can be developed 
this way and if someone wants to sell software and someone else wants to 
buy it, then I don't see any difference between that and buying and 
selling anything else.  I don't believe the FSF is in favour of doing 
away with selling things altogether.  (If they are, I'll find out where 
some FSF programmers live and see if I can use anything they have in 
their living room.  If they think of home security the way they think of 
system security that shouldn't be a problem:)

They also contaminate the discussion by harking back to the `good old
days' when there was zero-security unix and everyone could read everyone
else's stuff, answer other people's mail and so on.  Security is a
separate issue and is necessary in many contexts.  The problem is, the
portray the development of system security as a means of the restricting
the availability of software by the guys who want to charge money for
it.  The two issues are actually quite separate and I think that by 
mixing them they create a wrong impression and are missing the point.

Again, free software is fine.  It would just be nicer if some of the 
people producing it, like the FSF, would stop putting down all they 
people who program for a living.  I use some good free software and some 
good paid-for software.  Bad free software and bad paid-for software 
also exist.  I use the right tool for the job, whether or not it's free 
is secondary.  (Of course, if I can't buy all I want, it's nice that 
some stuff is free, but I'm not naive enough to think that making 
everything free is a viable economical model.)

Check out 

   http://www.gnu.ai.mit.edu/philosophy/stallman-kth.html

or

   http://www.gnu.ai.mit.edu/philosophy/philosophy.html

for some rantings.  Let me just pick one example.  Studies are cited
comparing gnu and proprietary unix stuff, gnu comes out on top (I agree)
and this is interpreted as `free better than proprietary in general'.  I
think it shows how bad proprietary unix software can be.  Just consider:
no VMS system manager would dream of replacing something in SYS$SYSTEM
with something some students hacked in their spare time! 


