In article <34EEA1DC.366EFFF8@sgi.com>,
	Gints Klimanis <gints@sgi.com> writes:
   Tony Griffiths wrote:
   > The VMS development team was one of the most disciplined I have
   > ever seem.  Coding was left strickly to the professionals.  'Raw'
   > recruits (ie. college grads) DID NOT get their sticky little
   > fingers on the code pool.  Their first job was bug fixing under
   > the guide of an experienced mentor.  Only when they had proved
   > themselves did they get to work on new development.

   I don't know exactly when this work started.  However, it's clear
   that college students of the 90's and today have an attribute
   missing from college graduates of earlier years: an additional
   decade of exposure to computers.  Although one can question the
   value of that experience, that additional time on personal
   computers should not be ignored.  A mid 80's high school graduate
   entering engineering school had probably written just as much code
   as a mid 80's college graduate.

Indeed, that time probably _should_ be ignored, because that extra
time very well may be to their detriment.  Much of the time I consider
most productive in my formative years as a programmer was while I
didn't even have access to a computer.  Instead, I wrote code - and
then reviewed the code, and then reviewed the code, and then reviewed
the code.  By the time I _did_ have access to a computer (usually
after summer vacation), the code was tight and mostly bug-free.  I'm
not saying this as an elder's "when I was young" contest, either - I'm
still young, and I actually _enjoyed_ it at the time.  I still have
enforced periods during which things get done in longhand.

Too many of the people I recall from college and college age people
I've met recently think that lists and trees are things provided by
the operating system, and have absolutely no comprehension of the work
that someone has put in to provide the components they so blithely put
together using a UI builder.  They think that things just happen.
Data structures don't even blip their radar.  In some cases, that
isn't a big problem.  But it does lead directly to the "All I have is
a hammer, everything must be a nail" problem.  There is a definite
inability to think outside the box.

Worse, they don't seem to have much if any notion of the compromises
you have to make to get acceptable performance.  Put another way, for
most students the section on big-O notation might as well have been
spent in a bar for all they remember.  I've seen some pretty scary
results from projects where someone has hacked various vendor-supplied
objects to accomplish a result that would be more easily (and cleanly)
done with some custom code.


Beyond all that, though, nothing gives you a better appreciation for a
fine piece of software than a deep understanding of what you would
have to do to implement that software yourself.  And nothing is more
annoying than seeing a badly done piece of software when you
understand the work necessary to implement it well enough to know that
it didn't have to be that way,
--
