Dag-Erling Coidan Sm�rgrav <dag-erli@ifi.uio.no> wrote:
>Honestly, you're complaining that your program is crashing when you're
>doing something illegal?
>
>The OS is *doing you a favor* by sending your program a SIGFPE, which
>causes it to abort and produce a coredump, which you can then use for
>debugging.  If the division by zero were silently ignored, you'd never
>know there had been an error, and you might actuallly rely on the
>(incorrect) result the program would produce.

Well, this depends on how you look at it.  IEEE math goes to a whole lot of
trouble to handle this kind of thing in a semi-useful way (the error isn't
silently ignored, it gets propogated precisely so that you *don't* end up
with random results the way you would on older proprietary FP systems),
it seems silly to let the C RTS defeat that since otherwise IEEE math is no
better than the chaos that preceeded it.

John Wilson
