No new versions for sometime ?
I have been receiving new versions of VMS every 6 to 12 months for the last
couple of years. True I was hoping VMS 7.2 would be out by now and it looks
like it will be the summer before it ships.
These weren't just bug fixes but some major releases  for instance :-

VMS 7.1 for Alpha includes :-

Very Large Memory support (VLM)
Kernel Threads
Upgraded X windows to X11 R6  - Motif with CDE
Lan Emulation over ATM network
and Support for ATM adapters
API for the BACKUP utility
Memory channel support
Upgraded SCSI drivers to support upto 16 devices per SCSI bus
External user Authentication 
Addition of UNIX style input/output redirection, pipelining facilities


In VMS 7.2 I am looking forward to a new ( but totally compatible ) file system
which will provide :-

Unicode filenames
Much longer filenames ( I can't remember the exact figure - maybe 512
characters )
More than eight levels of directory trees ( limited I think by 4096 character limit
of file specification).
Filenames which can contain multiple dots.
Snappy disks - facility which allows perfect online backups ( by taking a
snapshot of the disk which can be backed up while users continue to use the
disk and which does not utilise large amount of diskspace ).

Note. These features are designed not to upset applications written for the
current file system.


Also of course in the pipeline  :-

Galaxies ( the successor to clusters )
and
Wildfire - a machine architecture to fully take advantage of galaxies.

These will appear on VMS first - possibly with later ports to NT.

These are the things I know are planned for the near future - there will
probably a lot more in VMS 7.2 which I haven't heard about yet.

True the longer term future will probably depend on COMPAQ but most of the
things mentioned above are either already complete ( ie in beta for VMS 7.2 )
or well on the way.



David Webb
VMS and UNIX systems Manager
Middlesex University
 



