From: Volker Barthelmann <volker@vb.franken.de>
Subject: Re: Size of int on Alpha
Date: 1998/03/05
Message-ID: <6dkpic$33l@vb.franken.de>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 331036436
References: <34FD104F.B9FCF863@atlantis-consulting.com>
Organization: home
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch


Sivaram Krishnan <sivaram@atlantis-consulting.com> wrote:


: 1) What is the size of int (sizeof(int)) on an Alpha system running
:     Windows NT?

Never tried on NT, but I'm pretty sure it's 4.

: If it is 32-bits or 4-bytes, what is the result of the following
: operation?

:     int i = 0x80000000;  /* left most bit alone set */
:     int j;

:     j = i << 1;                /* left shift i by 1 */

: What is the value of j?

Should be 0.

: Since Alpha is a 64-bit architecture and since it does not
: support 32-bit shifts (Alpha has only 64-bit shift instructions),
: does the compiler mask the upper 4 bytes using extra instructions?

Actually, in this case you can use a 32bit add to perform the shift.
In other cases the compiler has to insert extra instructions, yes.

: This would seem expensive.  If the size of int is always
: 8 bytes, this problem does not arise.

It's not terribly expensive. Compilers don't have to insert those
instructions always. They usually try to keep track of what's in the
upper 32bits of a register and only insert them when necessary.

With sizeof(int)==8 the memory usage would be higher. Pretty hard to
say which would be better in what cases. Also I assume 64bit ints would
break lots of Windows-code.

: 2) Is the size of int different on Unix systems on Alpha?

They use sizeof(int)==4 and sizeof(long)==8. AFAIK NT uses sizeof(long)==4.

Volker






