Bill Moyer <billm@cygnus.com> wrote:

>  There have already been some halfhearted attempts to solve
>the problems of increasing program complexity by application
>of object-oriented techniques, but the more I see the object
>oriented style fail to live up to its promises, [...]

I'm constantly amazed at the way the OO paradigm has taken
over so many minds.  In my experience it's great for toy
programs, but gives you a complexity blow-out on any
reasonable-sized project.  Admittedly a lot of my OO experience
comes in conjunction with using the Microsoft Foundation
Class library, which seems to have been designed expressly
for the purpose of convincing people that OO libraries are
evil.  Nevertheless, I am still not convinced that there's
a good way to use OO techniques in conjunction with clean
programming practices.

> [...]  Use of memory segmentation would help pin-
>point which data structure (or catagory of data structures)
>was corrupted, and allow recovery routines to perhaps repair 
>the damage and continue with application execution; this 
>would be a big improvement over "Segmentation Fault; core 
>dumped" or "GPF 314159".  

One of my major gripes about C is the strong influence it's
had on operating systems design.

We have memory segmentation.  We've had it for years.  What
we don't have are operating systems that make proper use
of the segmentation hardware.

It's true that the earliest attempts to introduce segmentation
were killed off by considerations of hardware cost and speed.
But those problems were solved some years ago, when the
80286 first appeared.  Once you can get the segmentation
hardware onto the processor chip, and have some space left over
for decent-sized caches, you've got the technology to bring
segmentation to the masses at an affordable cost.

The catch is that you have all those C programmers demanding
a "linear address space".  Why would anyone need a linear
address space?  Answer: it's needed if you want to do dirty
tricks with pointer arithmetic.  Never mind that those dirty
tricks fell out of favour years ago.  The C standard says
that they're legal, so any C compiler that doesn't support
them is not standard-compliant.

For the more modern programming languages, a segmented
address space is superior.  There's a natural mapping between
module boundaries and segment boundaries.  Unfortunately,
our compilers tend not to take advantage of this, because of
the bias in the operating systems support towards linear
addressing.

Indeed, the segmentation hardware is not taking off the way
one would have expected.  Even Intel, which must have invested
a fortune in developing their segmentation hardware, has
dropped the idea.  They keep it in their 80x86 line for
backwards compatibility, but they're not putting segmentation
hardware into their other processors.  Presumably they
decided that the demand just wasn't there.

>  [...] and we can do a lot better than C.

Perhaps you hadn't noticed the newsgroups to which this was
cross-posted.  Most of us are already doing a lot better 
than C.

