The 8086 and 80286 employ segmentation as a hack to increase memory
address space. It is totally inadequate as a vehicle for providing the
benefits of a true segmented archetecture.

> Once you can get the segmentation
>hardware onto the processor chip, and have some space left over
>for decent-sized caches, you've got the technology to bring
>segmentation to the masses at an affordable cost.

Perhaps, but it is the x86 archetecture that has given segmentation a bad
name, not any specific programming language.

>The catch is that you have all those C programmers demanding
>a "linear address space".  Why would anyone need a linear
>address space?  Answer: it's needed if you want to do dirty
>tricks with pointer arithmetic.  Never mind that those dirty
>tricks fell out of favour years ago.  The C standard says
>that they're legal, so any C compiler that doesn't support
>them is not standard-compliant.

ANSI C is designed to work in a segmented archetecture, it in no way
requires a linear address space. It is invalid in ANSI C to perform
pointer arithmetic beyond the bounds of the current object (except a
one-past-the-end-rule which isn't a major issue). == and != can be used
to compare pointers into different objects but not > >= < or <=. Every
variable you define and every object you allocate with malloc/calloc/realloc
could be placed in a different segment and that would be a valid ANSI C
implementation. The standard was quite carefully crafted to make an
efficient implemenattion of the language possible on a segmented
archetecture.

>For the more modern programming languages, a segmented
>address space is superior.  There's a natural mapping between
>module boundaries and segment boundaries.  Unfortunately,
>our compilers tend not to take advantage of this, because of
>the bias in the operating systems support towards linear
>addressing.

