Joel Gallun <joel@gwyn.tux.org> writes:

> phkahler@oakland.edu (Paul Kahler) writes:
> 
> > The linux kernel was written by talented programmers. It's the 300,000
> > line program that got written in 30,000 :-)
> 
> /usr/src/linux> (find . -name '*.[chSs]' -exec cat {} \;)|wc -l
>  815720
> 
> Looks like you are off by an order of magnitude. Linux 2.0.33

That isn't a fair metric; it counts all architectures, and all possible drivers.
I propose this:

	/usr/src/linux> for a in arch/* [b-z]* ; do echo -n $a ; (find $a -name '*.[chSs]' -exec cat {} \;)|wc -l ; done

to obtain the followint result (2.0.32)

arch/alpha	12062
arch/i386	29414
arch/m68k	59494
arch/mips	 7705
arch/ppc	10939
arch/sparc	21771
drivers	       446492
fs		68177
include		43496
init		 1078
ipc		 2405
kernel		 7282
lib		 1829
mm		 7051
net		56845
scripts		 5359

So, we see that the majority of code is in hardware drivers, with
significant chunks in handling foreign filesystems (18 are supported
by Linux!---win(4)/mac/os2/bsd/cdrom/nfs) network, etc.

If we count the 'microkernel' part (kernel, memory management, ipc,
initialization, ext2 filesystem, and architecture from say i386,
excluding the math coprocessor emulation) is around 35-40 kLOC, for
whatever it is worth.

