In article <6dcdvp$6oa$1@mainsrv.main.nc.us>,  <scott@softbase.com> wrote:
>The best refutation of proofs for programs is in Peter van der Linden's
>Deep C Secrets. He shows a page from such a proof, which is
>incomprehensible to anyone other than a theoretical mathematician, and
>then describes a Usenet flame war about an error in a proof.
>
>There are not enough programmers to go around, and requiring
>mathematicians to prove programs on top of them will never work. The
>only hope we have is "black box" object programming, where an object is
>just a plug-in component. It will either work reliably, or can be
>replaced if it does not.

But over time, you could create libraries of formally proven black
boxes. The time required to do the proof is worthwhile if countless
people can benefit from it.

I don't believe your claim that such proofs are comprehensible only to
theoretical mathematitican. Anyone with a background in computer science
should be able to follow such a proof.   The proofs usually involve simple
predicate logic, and simple algebra.

There exist excellent textbooks on formal verification which can provide a
refresher course. Knowing the techniques can help you write more solid code
even in situations where you don't apply the techniques, because they
give you a framework for reasoning about program logic.



