In article <6d8skf$dtm$1@probity.mcc.ac.uk>, helbig@multivac.jb.man.ac.uk (Phillip Helbig) writes:
:How does one physically connect the machines (cables, ports etc)?

  One usually uses Ethernet -- often ThinWire -- to connect these boxes.

:What is the oldest VMS VAX version supported for clustering with VMS 7.1 
:ALPHA?  

  Check the Software Product Description (SPD) for details -- I'd tend
  to recommend V6.2 be the oldest version, given the availability of the
  V7.1 compatibility kit for that version.  (One can run in a VMScluster
  with other versions, but the new mount and the new shadowing are the
  best combination -- both are present in V7.1, and available for V6.2
  with the CLUSIO and associated ECO kits.)

:Can the two VAX machines share a system disk, or does each machine have
:to have some (perhaps minimal) system disk of its own?  

  OpenVMS VAX systems can share a system disk.

:Can the VAX machines access the CD-ROM drive in my ALPHAstation?

  Yes.  For installation and bootstrap and the occasional firmware
  updates (yes, some VAX platforms have firmware updates), the drive
  is best local, however.  I'd swap it over as needed when performing
  these operations -- one can use it "served" the rest of the time.

:What about the diskette drive (which is not SCSI)?

  I do not believe the floppy drives are served -- this was a
  deliberate decision, as it avoided having multiple devices with
  the same name in the same allocation class.  One can access the
  (remote) OpenVMS VAX disks from the AlphaStation, which can access
  the (local) floppy.

:Is it possible/desirable to have an ethernet address (and hence direct 
:accessibility over the net) for just one machine?

  Huh?  Each Ethernet is unique.  Are you refering to an IP address?

:Can I quickly change, unknown to the outside world, which machine has 
:the one ethernet address (and name)?

  IP addresses can be a bit painful to swap around -- see the OpenVMS
  FAQ for what is involved in changing the nodename -- and I'd tend to
  leave them static.  One can also use the IP cluster alias feature,
  and have one IP name and address that refers to the whole VMScluster.

:Which machine should be the main cluster machine?

  Usually the biggest and fastest...  In your case, I'd (continue) to
  use the AlphaStation as the core node.

	--

  Do not exceed a system disk capacity of 1.073 GB on any member of
  the VAXstation 3100 series.  Data disks can be larger.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
