>This whole thread amuses me greatly. I finnished high school in 1987
>with more programming experience than a mid 90's college graduate, 

The problem with undergraduate programs in CS is that they cannot assume 
you know anything about computers when first you start.  Imagine if 
degrees in English couldn't assume you knew how to read before hand:
they'd have to spend 3 years desperately teaching you to read, starting
with "See Dick run", and then in the last year you could read either Hamlet 
or Moby Dick.

There's a lot more to CS than sorting lists (just like there's a lot more
to English than "See Dick run").  Some examples of CS Theory I've had to
pick up on my own include:
1) Compiler theory (usefull to anyone parsing text files)
2) OS theory (usefull for anyone writing system level programs, or
multi-threaded applications).
3) Numerical Analysis (usefull to anyone who uses floating point)
4) Computer Graphics
5) Cryptography
6) Database theory (usefull to anyone managing large data structures)

The problem is that, if you don't know the appropriate theory, you don't
recognize when it's applicable to the problem you're trying to solve.

>and
>yes, all that experience was in a very un-disciplined environment. The
>college courses taught me some discipline and the ability to write more
>structured code. We also learned how to analyze the efficiency of
>algorithms, but never anything about how to devise one for a task you
>can't read about in a book! 

It's surprising what you can just read from a book, if you read the right
