Pete Becker wrote:
> 
> Terry Moore wrote:
> >
> > That is not the way to use proofs of programs. It's much easier
> > to develop the proof along with the program, so it's the
> > programmer's task, not the mathematician's.
> 
> Which does not change the preceding fact. The issue is not whether these
> things take time, but how much time they take. In order for this
> approach to be cost effective you need to show that proving correctness
> requires less time than fixing defects, which you have not done.

It will be pretty damn difficult to show that in a general case, however
a simple look at practical software should suffice ;-). I think there is
literature that indicates practical gains of up to 40% in total
devellopment time, however.

Terry is right, and I'd like to follow up that proving the correctness
of a program is quite simple as long as a program is develloped using
stepwise refinement and using logical abstractions. I think this is what
Terry means when he says 'along with the program'. Split the program
into enough modules and procedures for which pre- and postconditions are
clear, and you'll end up with trivial proof for these procedures. Now,
by way of induction, it will be possible to prove the entire program. 

You'll need a decent programming language to make this statement valid
though! I can tell you in Modula-2 it can be done. Once a program proven
this way compiles it will be error-free. It can be done in C too, but
there you'll be on your own; the compiler won't help you.

I'll admit however, that it is not as simple as that, since there is
allways the human factor, creating typo's, errors while copying the
algorithms etc. But these errors are quite easily spotted in trivial
procedures. 

So, making a point that proof is (or can be) trivial, you will easily
deduct that the actual time it takes to proof is neglectable, certainly
when compared to the time wasted on debugging.

