You have to realize that Microsoft as a company has only done two things
well in 15+ years.

1. they made excellent compilers from the early years even considering the
   primitive capacities of the early PC's; I still think their compilers
   are among the best,

2. they can market anything (at least to the 90's consumer - it is not
   clear that those raised during the Depression would have been so
   stupid as to repeatedly send good money after bad).

However, we as consumers have some alternatives. In my own discipline the
journal far prefer LATeX (or TeX) formatted documents and you can get 
these free for your Alpha. There is even a TeXCAD available.

Warn your less computer literate friends that Microsoft software is
overpriced and has a proven history of non-deterministic failures. Tell
them that the "Find" never finds anything (unless it is trivial). Tell
them that basically the whole Microsoft Software paradigm is to
be "In Your Face" with menus popping up everywhere. If you don't they
will think the failures they are getting are their fault (which is
exactly what Microsoft's marketeers want them to think).


