> I take "I think" to mean that he has seen such literature but
> cannot give a reference off hand. Well, nor can I, but there
> must be a better way to program than the try-it-and-hope-it-works
> principle that is used so much now.

Yes I have seen literature, No I do not have it at hand.

So, from my own expierience I can say that:

Doing a 190KByte program in Borland C++ (not the worst of devellopment
environments I know) took me 3 months.

Doing the same program from scratch WITH decent stepwise refinment and
on the fly proof for correctness in TopSpeed M2 (Almost as good as
Turbo) took me 3 weeks.

In fact, it took as much time to type in version A as it did to type
version B. However, once that was done, it took me 7 weeks with version
A to test (and debug) and 3 days with version B. In fact, after 3 weeks
of testing A) we started thinking of throwing the whole damn thing away
and start anew. It took further 3 more weeks to finaly take that
decision, i.e. as long as it took to redo the entier program!

OK, so I AM more fluent in M2 than C++. Still I know at least two other
companies in my direct vincinity, that based on lenghty discussions with
the software division of my company on programming techniques in
general, have adopted a more formal approach to programming, and they
have achieved comparable results. One (advanced image recognition)
whilst switching from c to pascal, the other (workstation CAD/CAM)
decided to stick to C++.

However, all three of us have concluded that the number of bugs found at
runtime (i.e. when the product is being tested, or even when installed
on site with their customers) calculated for more than 60% of the total
time for devellopment of the product (i.e. from time of conception of
the algorithm to time of delivery) and this can be reduced to approx 10%
of total time once proper proving was performed. 

Yes, there were STILL bugs left, because, as stated earlier, the human
factor cannot be ruled out. However, these bugs were mostly due to using
mixing up variables or forgetting to properly initialize them. Some
compilers would have warned of the latter one, bound-checking catched
the others within a couple of minutes.

As one of our staff uses to say: Once I find one or two bugs, I can
asume it's the odd mistake. Once I've found nr. 10, I can no longer
assume that there will be no more, in fact I now know for sure that
there will always be one more, and the entier program has to be
seriously questioned.

-- 
Ruud Bruijnesteijn
SDS Sport.Data.Systems GmbH
http://www.sds-sportdata.com



