>You both forgot another HUGE mistake - wasting all that time and effort on
>OSI instead of TCP/IP. VMS could have had RMS transparent tcpip file access
>many years ago - it would have ruled the internet.


Well, DEC is hard at work reimplementing OSI, except this time its got a new
name: IPv6.

OSI is in fact the replacement for "TCP/IP", required by government policy
just a few
years ago.  However, there were only a couple of computer companies that
actually
write code, as opposed to just shipping code written by comp sci students,
so most
computer companies couldn't manage to implement a decent OSI stack.  So, the
government caved in and reversed more than a decade of government policy
whose purpose was to implement a protocol stack that would work for the long
term.

If you want a head start on understanding IPv6, start studying DECnet PLUS.
The format of addresses, auto-configuring addresses, auto-configuring
routing,
automatic registration in distributed name servers, and even managing dual
protocol stacks.
And if you managed a migration from DECnet to DECnet OSI and thought that
was
painful because you had to replace all your routers, learn a lot of new
things, etc.

Just wait until you have to deal with migrating 10 to 100 times as many
devices
as you did with DECnet.  Upgrading a company with hundreds of Win 3.1
systems
to Win 95 and WinNT will seem trivial, in comparison.

Oh, and by the way, RMS remote access does work over TCP/IP, shared file,
indexed and
relative, and all the rest.





