Sean Burke wrote:
>
>Sean> system is so popular today. Perhaps our code will in turn be
>Sean> described as "lean-n-mean" by those who follow us. But don't
>Sean> bet on it. In the Mac world, the term "byte-squeaking pinheadery"
>Sean> has been applied to some of the brilliant coding that enabled
>Sean> the original MacOS to run in 128K.

  I occasionally wonder if (or when) software will become suf-
ficiently massive and complex that system performance will be 
dominated by the frequency and time it takes to recover from 
application (or system) crashes.  If this happened, upgrading 
to faster hardware would not effectively improve productivity.

  There have already been some halfhearted attempts to solve
the problems of increasing program complexity by application
of object-oriented techniques, but the more I see the object
oriented style fail to live up to its promises, the more I
think that techniques discarded in the past for being slow, 
inconvenient, or impractical (memory segmentation with bounds 
checking, strong type checking, compile-time proving of code
correctness, et al) might be worth another look.  Run-time 
bounds checking increases the processor power required to run
applications, but applications which are bottlenecked by crash
frequency (eg, programmer error in pointer arithmetic) would 
benefit from it.  Use of memory segmentation would help pin-
point which data structure (or catagory of data structures)
was corrupted, and allow recovery routines to perhaps repair 
the damage and continue with application execution; this 
would be a big improvement over "Segmentation Fault; core 
dumped" or "GPF 314159".  

  Relying on run-time capture and correction of errors bugs
the hell out of me.  Like most programmers, I believe that
programming errors should be caught and corrected before a
product ships, but the current practice of shipping products
with dozens or hundreds of *known* bugs does not show any 
sign of changing.  

  Changing the programming language to improve compile-time
verification of code correctness (with richer and stronger
types, richer static semantic sets, and compile-time range 
tracking) would locate more bugs at compile-time, reduce the 
number of bugs in a "finished" product, and reduce the overall 
time spent in the development cycle (because finding run-time 
bugs is a much longer and more laborious task than finding 
them at compile-time).  It has been proven that the imperative 
languages are not provable (ie, for any turing-complete imper-
ative language, a program can be written which is not provably 
correct or incorrect), but it is still possible to improve 
provability by degrees, and we can do a lot better than C.

  -- Bill Moyer



