> verification of code correctness (with richer and stronger
> types, richer static semantic sets, and compile-time range
> tracking) would locate more bugs at compile-time, reduce the
> number of bugs in a "finished" product, and reduce the overall
> time spent in the development cycle (because finding run-time
> bugs is a much longer and more laborious task than finding
> them at compile-time).  It has been proven that the imperative
> languages are not provable (ie, for any turing-complete imper-
> ative language, a program can be written which is not provably
> correct or incorrect), but it is still possible to improve
> provability by degrees, and we can do a lot better than C.

Hmm. I think you're right from a pure theoretical point of view, and in
fact practice is that most programs are impossible to prove. However,
the fact that programs can be written that can not be proven, does not
exclude the possibility that there are programs that can! It's  'a
discipline of programming' ;-)

Algorithms can be proven, (if you can't prove it, it's not an
algorithm!) but the step from algorithm to implementation (the
'mapping') is a sore point. But you're right that all a strickt
language can do to help is to improve the chances to catch an error.

However, Did you take look at the work by Mishra and Mani Chandri in
Austin on the provability of software, and the formal descriptional
method
they call UNITY. Have you looked at Component Pascal (Oberon-2,
BlackBox) for a real neat modern approach? It's not perfect, but it sure
is a step in the right direction, I think.
 
