Piercarlo Grandi wrote:
[...] 
> The Microsoft UNIX product was called Xenix (as at that time only AT&T
> and not its licensees could use the original name), and they had ported
> it to a number of machines (notably 68000 based and Intel based).
> 
> SCO Xenix, on which SCO built their fortune, was licensed from
> Microsoft, and I would not be surprised if SCO were still paying
> royalties to Microsoft.

More info, from:

UNIX Systems: Advanced Administration and Management Handbook, by 
Bruce and Karen Hunter, Macmillan  ISBN 0-02-358950-7 
QA76.76.063H86 (1991) 

p5 "The Santa Cruz Operation put Microsoft's UNIX version, XENIX, 
on the map by selling and supporting the system with such vigor 
that there are currently more XENIX machines than any other UNIX 
version." [n.b., this was 1991] 

p183 "Believe it or not, at this writing there are more active 
XENIX systems than any other UNIX version.  Few UNIX systems can 
be installed and set up as easily as SCO XENIX can.  The 
following is how you set up an Intel 301 80386 PC running SCO 
Xenix System V.  The hardware consists of two Seagate 40-megabyte 
hard drives 2.5 megabytes of 32-bit memory, and an 80387 math 
coprocessor." [I'm impressed -- again, book was written in 1991] 

I recall Microsoft's XENIX adverts back in 1985 or 1986; a long 
line of cave people was marching towards, and in awe of, a 
monolithic stone carving of "XENIX" (probably a brother of the 
warrior princess).  XENIX was fairly popular for distributed 
inventory control systems, and was used at our local hospital. In 
1986, one of the local secretarial services had 30 terminals 
connected to a Compaq DeskPro 386 running XENIX; the users 
typically ran WordPerfect, and claimed they got more than 
adequate speed from the single DeskPro.

I also recall the AT&T experts, in charge of the grand SVR4
sysnthesis, were quite shocked to find XENIX outsold all other
UNIX versions.  My early 1992 SVR4 distributions were peppered with
special XENIX libraries and compatability information, and as
Piercarlo says, the XENIX acknowledgments were abundant in the
boot-up.

A new topic: what is the half-life of such arcane information, in 
the current computer community?  Or, an alternative title:  When 
did I become old?



