	12 was a custome, lab oriented 8. (Linc 8 derivative).
	9/15 were an 18 bit family.
	8/12 were a 12 bit family.
	11 was the first of the 16 bit family, totally new byte orient
	architecture.  11/20 was first, expandiong downwards to 11/05, upwards
	to 11/70 and, arguably, Vax 11/780 (the original moniker, as i
	recall...), but also the start of a newsih 32 bit architecture.

>> After the 12 was the 14.
	12 was a modifed 8, a 12 bit machine.
	PDP 14 was a PLC (Programmable Logic Controller), with no predecessors,
	and arguably the PDP 16 as a successor.

>> after the 14 was the 15.
	PDP 14 was a PLC (as above).
	The PDP 15 was an 18 bit arcitecture, successor to the PDP 9.

>> There was something referred to as the 16.
	A family of logic modules, intended for PLC like use.

>> There was something referred to as the 18.
	Personal Fuzzy_neuron Alarm.  I think so.  I ferget...

====================
The PDP numbering system was non monotonic, covering disjoint
36, 18, 12 and 16 bit architectures, and just sniffing at the 32 Bit.

