If you haven't noticed, everything has serious bugs today. I personally
buy networking gear. It usually does *not* work as expected. Even 
hubs ( the most simple device you can buy ) have bugs today. Hubs have bugs
that force special settings to the workstations, or make some LAN cards
unusable... and these kind of hubs are from a Well Known Big Company.. not el
cheapo noname boxes.

It is actually quite simple. A company makes a new product, and it starts to
work, sort of. 

They analyse it.. we want to start selling NOW.. how much does it cost to make this
feature really work ? How long does it take How many % of customers will be hurt ? 

If they think they can make money with the broken product, they
start selling it and are prepared to handle the unhappy customers in
a way or another. If they are big enough, they don't really care about
the small customers. Big customers will get service
engineers/programmers that keep them happy enough. For example M$
has special priests that run around fixing things for the Important
Customers.


